\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  to keep stronger  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  next month-source\b0\par\par\uc2 392 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2014\par 00:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds background on rollover rate of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps)\par \par \uc2 By Patricia Duarte\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Sept 25 (Reuters) - Brazilian policymakers are likely to roll over 100 percent of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps that expire in November in a bid to curb a sharp depreciation of the real, a source on President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2 's economic team said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "There is a very reasonable possibility," the source told Reuters when asked whether the bank would keep next month its increased rollover pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, derivatives designed to support the real.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday announced it would intensify its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the real closed weaker than 2.4 per dollar for the first time in seven months, hit by uncertainty about the outcome of the October presidential elections.\par \par \uc2 It increased to 100 percent from about 75 percent the rollover rate of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps that expire in October. In previous months, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had systematically allowed some swaps to expire.\par \par \uc2 Despite the bank's efforts, the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slid further on Thursday, closing at a seven-month low of 2.4294 per dollar, as investors feared higher interest rates in the United States as well as electoral polls in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 At these levels, "it begins to get dangerous" for those investors who are shorting the real, the source warned, explaining that any scenario reversal could cause a sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjustment that could impose large losses to investors.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian financial markets have been selling off whenever President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2  gains support in opinion polls. Investors accuse her of failing to curb inflation and driving the economy into a recession.\par \par \uc2 The source, who spoke on condition of anonymity, said policymakers do not plan to increase the daily supply of 4,000 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps under a program that is set to continue at least until the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Instead, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to offer dollars through repurchase agreements at the end of the year, when the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market usually needs a liquidity boost.\par \par \uc2 There are no plans to offer dollar lines before that as the spot dollar market remains "liquid" despite its increased volatility, the source said. (Reporting by Patricia Duarte, writing by Walter Brandimarte; Editing by Diane Craft and David Gregorio)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-9-28T22:41:15.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140925ea9p00q5p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
