\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Becomes an Unlikely Star in Presidential Campaign\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paulo Trevisani   \par \uc2 888 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2014\par 00:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BRAS\u205\'00\'CDLIA--\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governance isn't an issue most politicians waste much breath on, but Brazilian voters are getting an earful in the midst of a tight presidential race.\par \par \uc2 With the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil\uc2  under fire for failing to stem inflation, the main candidates have come out swinging on who should control the institution.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is part of the Finance Ministry, though it has a special status and is largely autonomous. The bank's chief, \uc2 Alexandre Tombini\uc2 , has the title of minister and reports directly to President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2 . \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors currently serve at the will of the president and have no fixed terms.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Rousseff defends this relationship as necessary to keep the bank focused on policies beneficial to all Brazilians, not just investors and the financial industry.\par \par \uc2 But with inflation hovering at around 6.5% and eating into Brazilians' pocketbooks, Ms. Rousseff's main rival, Marina Silva of the Brazilian Socialist party, is stumping for an independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  similar to those in the U.S. and Europe.\par \par \uc2 The front-runner in recent polls, Ms. Silva wants to establish set terms for the bank's eight governors and restrict the president's power to fire them. The aim, her advisors say, is to give central bankers the ability to raise the benchmark Selic rate as much as needed to fight inflation, without government pressure.\par \par \uc2 The topic, eye-glazing to most, has made for a surprisingly lively debate, with both candidates accusing the other of kowtowing to special interests.\par \par \uc2 The Silva camp says the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't confronted inflation aggressively enough because it is concerned that additional rate increases would hurt growth and employment in an election year. After the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted the Selic by 3.75 percentage points between April 2013 and April 2014, it has kept the rate at 11%.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Silva's team has also lambasted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prop up the real against the dollar. They view this activity as a risky back-door move to fight inflation that could lead to a large and swift devaluation of \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ultimately ends.\par \par \uc2 "Monetary policy can't be based on political whims and moods," Maurico Rands, one of Ms. Silva's top advisors said at a public forum on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Rousseff's government denies it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decisions. She and her supporters have fought back with a television ad warning that an independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could become a tool of the financial industry.\par \par \uc2 The spot shows food vanishing from the dinner table of average Brazilians as bankers sitting in a dark room plot to raise interest rates. An ominous voiceover says \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  autonomy, as proposed by Ms. Silva, "would mean giving bankers great decision power over you and your family's life; the interest you pay, your job and even your salary."\par \par \uc2 The debate has struck a chord with some voters in a country where economic turmoil often vaults otherwise arcane issues into the headlines.\par \par \uc2 Ricardo Fernandes, a 25-year-old parking valet works just a couple of blocks from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's imposing headquarters in Bras\u237\'00\'EDlia. While unable to explain exactly how an independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would affect his life, Mr. Fernandes said TV coverage has convinced him it's a good idea.\par \par \uc2 "I support \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  autonomy," Mr. Fernandes said. "I think Marina is more likely to give autonomy to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Bras\u237\'00\'EDlia resident Andr\u233\'00\'E9 de Almeida said he, too, has seen headlines and TV spots and come to the opposite conclusion. The 28-year old business student said he opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  autonomy because "private banks will have too much influence over it, and banks tend to prefer higher interest rates."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Almeida wouldn't disclose who he'll vote for, but said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue wouldn't influence his decision.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Silva and other proponents of a fully independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  say Brazil's current system makes the bank vulnerable to political influence.\par \par \uc2 Former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Gustavo Franco said as much to The Wall Street Journal a few years ago. In an interview in New York, he said he was ousted from his post by then-President Fernando Henrique Cardoso in 1999 under political pressure.\par \par \uc2 At the time, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was being targeted by speculators, and Mr. Cardoso wanted to adopt a free-floating exchange rate to thwart them. Mr. Franco said he opposed the move since pegging the real to the dollar had been a key pillar of the government's plan to staunch hyperinflation earlier in the decade. A Cardoso spokesman declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Rousseff maintains that her administration doesn't interfere with the bank's rate decisions and that the de facto autonomy she grants to the bank is sufficient.\par \par \uc2 Talking to reporters Sunday, Ms. Rousseff said that independence is equal to making the \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  a "fourth power." She said that the bank doesn't prioritize job creation, thus independence "does indeed take food out" from workers' table, she said.\par \par \uc2 Jeffrey T. Lewis\uc2  contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 15, 2014 18:00 ET (22:00 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140915ea9f003tb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
