\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  to extend  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  program - gov't source\b0\par\par\uc2 305 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2014\par 23:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds background, byline)\par \par \uc2 By Patricia Duarte\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Sept 12 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will extend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program into 2015 and may increase the rollover pace of expiring \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps to curb volatility in the real, a person on President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2 's economic team said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The person told Reuters that the current \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, which includes the sale of as many as 4,000 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps per day to support the real, will not suffer changes this year.\par \par \uc2 The real on Friday closed 1.6 percent weaker at 2.3349 per dollar, its weakest level in nearly 10 months, as anxiety about the country's October presidential election added to an environment of rising risk aversion fueled by fears of higher U.S. interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slid more than 2 percent during the session after an opinion survey by pollster Ibope confirmed President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2  and environmentalist Marina Silva were neck-and-neck in a vote expected to go to a second round on Oct. 26.\par \par \uc2 While many investors strongly dislike the current government and regard Silva as their best choice for change, remarks by a senior Silva economic adviser contributed to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with Reuters, Alexandre Rands, an economist who helped draft Silva's economic plan, said her government would eliminate several economic distortions that have dragged the once-booming Brazilian economy into recession, including the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program.\par \par \uc2 Without that program, analysts say the real would be much weaker than it is now, adding to inflation that is already at the ceiling of the official government target range. (Reporting by Patricia Duarte, Writing by Walter Brandimarte; Editing by Chris Reese)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-9-15T21:18:09.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140912ea9c00q8h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
