\par\par \b \uc2 Beset by problems at home, Brazilian steel looks out at a hard world\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stephen Eisenhammer   \par \uc2 765 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 August 2014\par 20:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Aug 15 (Reuters) - Whichever way Brazil's steel industry looks it sees pain.\par \par \uc2 At home, the economy is on the brink of recession and demand for the world's most used metal is on the wane. Abroad, Brazilian exports are hampered by a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and global production overcapacity.\par \par \uc2 What is needed, steel executives say, is widespread reform, including higher infrastructure spending, lower taxes, and a weaker Brazilian real .\par \par \uc2 But although steel companies talk urgently of a state of crisis, and many analysts and economists agree, little change can be expected soon with a presidential election just two months away.\par \par \uc2 Last week, \uc2 Companhia Siderurgica Nacional\uc2  SA, known as CSN, the country's largest diversified steelmaker, posted a 96 percent drop in quarterly net income. "Only a crazy person would invest in Brazil right now," CSN Chief Executive \uc2 Benjamin Steinbruch\uc2  told a conference in Sao Paulo this week.\par \par \uc2 Strong words for a leader of an industry that remains fiercely nationalistic. The conference began with the national anthem and executives repeatedly stressed their patriotic credentials before sticking the knife in.\par \par \uc2 The problem for Brazil is that the suffering of its steel industry is both the result of a weakening economy, as demand from the construction and auto sectors drops, and a cause of economic weakness as the high price of steel stops companies investing.\par \par \uc2 HOME PAIN\par \par \uc2 In the past three years economic growth in Brazil has slowed to a crawl with a number of economists suggesting the country may have already fallen into recession. An economic activity index that measures the farming, industrial and services sectors fell in June for a fifth straight month.\par \par \uc2 The country's auto industry is on track for its biggest drop in sales in more than a decade, and inflation is leaning heavily against the upper limit of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target range.\par \par \uc2 Industry group Instituto A\u231\'00\'E7o Brasil expects steel production to fall 2.5 percent this year, with domestic sales down nearly 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Benjamin Baptista\uc2 , CEO of the Brazilan unit of global steelmaker \uc2 ArcelorMittal\uc2 , tried to find a silver lining when asked for his forecasts. "It's hard to be worse than it is now," he said.\par \par \uc2 With the situation at home severe, exporting seems the answer. Steelmakers loathe to reduce production levels because it results in higher costs, politically sensitive job losses and puts companies at a disadvantage if markets pick up.\par \par \uc2 But international markets are flooded with steel from China, where a rampant increase in production over past decades has dramatically outstripped demand as Chinese growth slows.\par \par \uc2 Both Usiminas, formally known as \uc2 Usinas Siderurgicas de Minas Gerais SA\uc2  and one of the largest steelmakers in the Americas, and CSN say they are looking to export more steel products but acknowledge margins could suffer.\par \par \uc2 The biggest problem is the strong real. Some economists estimate that given Brazil's current account deficit, the real should be at a level of between 2.7 and 3.0 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 On Friday it was trading at 2.27 to the dollar. \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly in the market to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep inflation under control.\par \par \uc2 Steelmakers expect the real to weaken through to the end of the year, offering some respite. But the exchange rate is not the only problem.\par \par \uc2 HAVE YOUR CAKE AND EAT IT\par \par \uc2 According to Sergio Leite, vice-president for sales at Usiminas, the high tax burden in Brazil makes the industry uncompetitive.\par \par \uc2 He cited a study from A\u231\'00\'E7o Brazil in 2012 that showed Brazilian steel had lower production costs than any of the world's five main producers: the United States, Germany, China, Russia and Turkey.\par \par \uc2 But "when this steel arrives in the hands of the consumer, with taxes added, it arrives as the most expensive," Leite said.\par \par \uc2 High taxes, however, are in essence the flip side of the protection from imports the industry enjoys through strict anti-dumping legislation. The steel industry, by asking for lower taxes but a continuing barrier to imports, wants its cake and eat it too.\par \par \uc2 But the sense that reforms are needed, and will be delivered by whoever wins the election, is gaining traction.\par \par \uc2 "There is now a consensus from various industrial sectors that reform is needed to improve the tax system, to simplify and reduce bureaucracy," said \uc2 Luciano Coutinho\uc2 , president of Brazil's powerful development bank, BNDES. (Additional reporting by Walter Brandimarte; Editing by Todd Benson; and Peter Galloway) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-8-15T19:33:01.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140815ea8f00az8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
