\par\par \b \uc2 Venezuela Faces Tough Decisions on  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Policy -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,297 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2014\par 19:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 13:47 EDT - GlobalSource Partners says Venezuela has 2 short-term windows to install \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform: next month with the opposition currently in disarray or January during the holiday hangover. In the September scenario, the firm says inflation would threaten to reach triple digits in 2014 with GDP contracting 4.0-4.8%. Regardless, "what seems feasible one day falls off the government's agenda the next day. Government officials send mixed signals on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and gasoline-price hikes, suggesting a strong pushback by the chavista left is strong while unfavorable survey results hamper Maduro's readiness to act." (ezequiel.minaya@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:28 EDT - Roberto Enriquez, head of Venezuelan opposition party Copei, says the government's talk of possibly raising domestic gasoline prices is "immoral." It's no secret, he notes, that at about 1c/gallon, gasoline prices are too cheap. But to cut the subsidy just as the government acknowledges tens of billions of dollars is lost to fraud, corruption and mismanagement shows officials are trying to punish the average Venezuelan for their failures, contends Enriquez. He adds the opposition coalition will challenge economic reforms in the run-up to regional elections slated for next year, which will be a test for President Maduro's tenure. (kejal.vyas@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:11 EDT - Treasurys maintain their price gains after fair demand for today's 10-year auction which was in-line with recent sales. The notes are sold at a 2.439% yield, the lowest since June 2013 and slightly higher than the 2.432% 10s traded at right before the sale. The bid-to-cover ratio was 2.83, above the average 2.7 of the past 8 auctions, and indirect bidding took up 47% of the $24B deal, the biggest percentage since May. That's a sign foreign buyers were lured by much higher bond yields than the likes of Germany and Japan. Direct bidding was 15%, versus a 17% average. The 10-year note is 4/32 higher, yielding 2.426%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 13:01 EDT - The reported death of Brazilian presidential candidate Eduardo Campos could mean that if he's replaced by running mate Marina Silva, a runoff becomes more likely, says BBH. It says she may be able to take more votes from incumbent Dilma Rousseff than Campos would have. The firm adds the reported death and resulting ballot change could dim the spotlight from Aecio Neves, who's been in second place in polls and viewed as the business-friendly candidate most likely to beat Rousseff. If the attention turns away from Neves, that could boost Rousseff's chances of winning. (nicole.hong@wsj.com; @nicole_hong)\par \par \uc2 12:53 EDT - Peru's Banco de Credito believes the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will leave its reference interest rate unchanged at 3.75% the rest of the year and cut commercial-bank reserve requirements to boost liquidity. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  made a quarter-point cut last month and stood pat last week despite weakening growth. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  last week cut its 2014 GDP-growth view to 4.1% from 5.2%, went to 5.8% from 6.1% on 2015. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:39 EDT - The significance for markets of Brazilian presidential candidate Eduardo Campos' reported death in a plane crash may not be clear for several days, Leme Investimentos equity analyst Joao Pedro Brugger says. Stocks initially fell on the news and the Brazilian real weakened, but both have since pared some of the earlier losses. "It's natural for the market to react abruptly to this kind of news, totally unexpected," Mr. Brugger said. The long-term outlook will likely depend on whether Mr. Campos' PSB party decides to withdraw from the race or put forth his running mate, Marina Silva. (paul.kiernan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:33 EDT - The Bank of England's move to talk down the market's near-term rate hike expectations speaks to a kind of freeloader dilemma for policymakers: who would want to be the first \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates? The first one will suck in all the excess capital that's desperate for yield, driving up that country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and having a more exaggerated impact on competitiveness than would come from rate hikes in more "normal" times. The rest of the world gets to freeload. In that sense, Britain is the first major economy that can truly relate to the "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" problems that emerging markets such as Brazil would complain of a few years ago. (michael.casey@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:24 EDT - A monthly survey of 150 sales managers by \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  finds demand for newly built homes fell both sequentially and from a year earlier in July, indicating the market continues to be muted amid limited consumer confidence and stringent mortgage-qualification standards. Of respondents, 25% reported better-than-expected July orders, down from 34% in June and 29% a year ago. Those saying orders missed estimates rose to 26% from 19% and 24%, respectively. Meanwhile, 21% reported increasing their use of sales incentives--free upgrades or financial assistance with closing costs--down from 25% in June but sharply higher than July 2013's 11%. (kris.hudson@wsj.com; @KrisHudsonWSJ)\par \par \uc2 12:20 EDT - Investors were already watching Brazil like a hawk before today's reports that Eduardo Campos, the candidate polling third in Brazil's October presidential election, has died in a plane crash. How markets react likely depends on what investors think this does to incumbent Dilma Rousseff's chances. Markets have been in 'anybody-but-Dilma' mode for months, a response to Brazil's sluggish growth and what some see as heavy-handed government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the economy. Investors bid up \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stock market as Dilma's poll numbers slid. Would Campos' death limit the chance that his party and Aceio Neves, No. 2 in the polls, will split the anti-Dilma vote? Brazil's stock index down 1.5% after holding near unchanged earlier. (matt.day@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:14 EDT - Reports that Brazilian presidential candidate Eduardo Campos has died in a plane crash triggered volatility in the Brazilian stock market, with the benchmark Ibovespa index recently down 1.2% at 55753 points. Shares of \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 , which have been particularly sensitive to election-related news given the Brazilian government's heavy hand in company decisions, were off 2.5% at BRL19.17. (paul.kiernan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:00 EDT - The most-interesting thing in Markit's (MRKT) first quarterly report as a public company wasn't its results, but commentary from CEO Lance Uggla on the slower-than-expected adoption of electronic trading in over-the-counter products, especially derivatives. Moves to electronic trading will mean lower revenues for parts of the firm's business, but the assumption is baked into MRKT's business plan. Even though new regulations are in place for both buy and sell-side participants in the US, there has been no big change in trading behavior, with Uggla saying participants were using loopholes to avoid a move. For example, taking an ordinary rate swap and giving it a forward starting date makes it a structured derivative, which is exempt from the rules. MRKT is down 1.8% at $25.70. (paul.davies@wsj.com; @PaulJDavies)\par \par \uc2 "Canada Markets Trying to Guess What's in the Black Box -- Market Talk," published at 10:05 a.m. ET incorrectly identified the month of the labor survey. Statistics Canada found an error in the report for July, not August.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 13, 2014 13:47 ET (17:47 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140813ea8d002x8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
