\par\par \b \uc2 Small Companies Taking More Time With Hires -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,408 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2014\par 18:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 12:55 EDT - Small US employers are taking nearly twice as long to fill job openings versus five years ago, a new report shows. Between January and May, businesses with fewer than nine employees took a mean 23.6 days to hire new workers, up from 12.8 days in 2009, according to an analysis of labor statistics by Dice Holdings. Openings at firms with 10-49 employees were filled in 20.4 days, up from 13. Compared to larger firms, recruiting by small employers shows "little or no recovery" in the years since the recession, the report said. The development "reveals much tightening of labor markets for smaller companies," says \uc2 Chicago Booth\uc2  economist Steven Davis. (angus.loten@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:47 EDT - Coal imports are having a small revival with U.S. power plants, but they're still far off their record pace, EIA says. Imports of thermal coal accounted for less than 1% of all U.S. consumption in 2013, down from as much as 3% in 2007. U.S. power plants used about 7 million tons of imported coal last year, up from about 5 million in 2012, when imports hit a 10-year low. They had peaked in 2007 at more than 30 million tons, but power plants that import coal have been some of the hardest hit by the move away from coal power. Many plants closed and the ones that remained open ran only 37% of the time in 2013, down by half from 2006, EIA says. (tim.puko@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:46 EDT - New-restaurant growth in the US has stagnated as consumers choose to increasingly eat at home. The number of new openings grew less than 1% in the spring versus a year earlier, according to data/advisory firm NPD, with the little growth there was coming from fast food. The number of new full-service restaurants, which includes casual- and fine-dining establishments, fell 1%. "Prior to the recession, when industry traffic was strong and money was more available, the industry expanded units rapidly," notes NPD's Greg Starzynski. "But in today's market, unit growth must be a calculated risk." (julie.jargon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:39 EDT - The significance for markets of Brazilian presidential candidate Eduardo Campos' reported death in a plane crash may not be clear for several days, Leme Investimentos equity analyst Joao Pedro Brugger says. Stocks initially fell on the news and the Brazilian real weakened, but both have since pared some of the earlier losses. "It's natural for the market to react abruptly to this kind of news, totally unexpected," Mr. Brugger said. The long-term outlook will likely depend on whether Mr. Campos' PSB party decides to withdraw from the race or put forth his running mate, Marina Silva. (paul.kiernan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:37 EDT - One of the biggest buyers of stocks remains companies themselves even as they continue to set record highs. And firms picked up the pace on announcing fresh plans last month. Share-repurchase authorizations rose 21% on-year to $59B, says Birinyi Associates. The research firm counts 87 authorizations, a 5% increase over last year, with Intel's (INTC) $20B plan leads the way dollarwise in July. But while the year-to-date total is down 16% at $384.3B, the number of companies announcing new or updated efforts has risen to 635 from 581. (dan.strumpf@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:33 EDT - It's another turning-point day for stocks with the Dow industrials' 90-point climb pushing it into positive territory for the year. Meanwhile, though the S&P 500 is up just 0.6%, all 10 sectors are in the green--and all save consumer discretionary is positive for 2014. If that segment can eke out another 0.2% climb, it will join the party. (michael.driscoll@wsj.com; @michaeldrisc)\par \par \uc2 12:33 EDT - The Bank of England's move to talk down the market's near-term rate hike expectations speaks to a kind of freeloader dilemma for policymakers: who would want to be the first \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates? The first one will suck in all the excess capital that's desperate for yield, driving up that country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and having a more exaggerated impact on competitiveness than would come from rate hikes in more "normal" times. The rest of the world gets to freeload. In that sense, Britain is the first major economy that can truly relate to the "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" problems that emerging markets such as Brazil would complain of a few years ago. (michael.casey@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:30 EDT - Buried in Tuesday's results from Candy Crush maker \uc2 King Digital Entertainment\uc2  (KING) was another reminder of why \uc2 Facebook\uc2  (FB) needs to move more strongly into the mobile payments market. The number of monthly active gamers fell 2% from the first quarter, which King attributed to a drop-off in people playing the game on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  on their desktop computers. \uc2 Facebook\uc2  earns 30% of revenues from transactions through its desktop payment platform -- but nothing from mobile, where developers use \uc2 Facebook\uc2  login tools for free. (lisa.fleisher@wsj.com, @lisafleisher)\par \par \uc2 12:30 EDT - Even as various surveys and anecdotal reports indicate homebuilders are using more sales incentives to boost volumes, the West Coast's \uc2 William Lyon Homes\uc2  (WLH) says its use of them remains relatively flat. Incentives--enticements such as free upgrades like premium flooring and financial assistance like helping buyers with closing costs--ranged from 0-4% of sales in 2Q and averaged 1.25%, roughly in line with previous results. But with WLH focusing on the higher end (2Q's average sales price surged 43% on-year to $500,500), that part of the market doesn't need incentives as much as entry-level buyers. While EPS beat consensus, shares are slightly lower today, putting the week's decline at 3.3%. (kris.hudson@wsj.com; @KrisHudsonWSJ)\par \par \uc2 12:26 EDT - \uc2 Barclays\uc2 ' new US stock-market strategist says buybacks are "on their way" to levels not seen since before the financial crisis. The bank estimates that companies use about 80% of their free cash flow after dividends to buy back their own shares. That's tacked on about 2% extra to EPS growth each year, firm says. "Now, there is nothing wrong with turning 2% revenue growth into 6% EPS growth," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says. But "we question if the market will pay peak multiples for engineered earnings." (alexandra.scaggs@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:24 EDT - A monthly survey of 150 sales managers by \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  finds demand for newly built homes fell both sequentially and from a year earlier in July, indicating the market continues to be muted amid limited consumer confidence and stringent mortgage-qualification standards. Of respondents, 25% reported better-than-expected July orders, down from 34% in June and 29% a year ago. Those saying orders missed estimates rose to 26% from 19% and 24%, respectively. Meanwhile, 21% reported increasing their use of sales incentives--free upgrades or financial assistance with closing costs--down from 25% in June but sharply higher than July 2013's 11%. (kris.hudson@wsj.com; @KrisHudsonWSJ)\par \par \uc2 12:20 EDT - Investors were already watching Brazil like a hawk before today's reports that Eduardo Campos, the candidate polling third in Brazil's October presidential election, has died in a plane crash. How markets react likely depends on what investors think this does to incumbent Dilma Rousseff's chances. Markets have been in 'anybody-but-Dilma' mode for months, a response to Brazil's sluggish growth and what some see as heavy-handed government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the economy. Investors bid up \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stock market as Dilma's poll numbers slid. Would Campos' death limit the chance that his party and Aceio Neves, No. 2 in the polls, will split the anti-Dilma vote? Brazil's stock index down 1.5% after holding near unchanged earlier. (matt.day@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com \par Cynthia Lin, 212 416-4403, Cynthia.lin@wsj.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181, john.shipman@wsj.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326, Patrick.sullivan@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 13, 2014 12:55 ET (16:55 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140813ea8d002xx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
