\par\par \b \uc2 DJIA Tiptoes Around Break-Even Level Again -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,422 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2014\par 18:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 12:33 EDT - It's another turning-point day for stocks with the Dow industrials' 90-point climb pushing it into positive territory for the year. Meanwhile, though the S&P 500 is up just 0.6%, all 10 sectors are in the green--and all save consumer discretionary is positive for 2014. If that segment can eke out another 0.2% climb, it will join the party. (michael.driscoll@wsj.com; @michaeldrisc)\par \par \uc2 12:33 EDT - The Bank of England's move to talk down the market's near-term rate hike expectations speaks to a kind of freeloader dilemma for policymakers: who would want to be the first \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates? The first one will suck in all the excess capital that's desperate for yield, driving up that country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and having a more exaggerated impact on competitiveness than would come from rate hikes in more "normal" times. The rest of the world gets to freeload. In that sense, Britain is the first major economy that can truly relate to the "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" problems that emerging markets such as Brazil would complain of a few years ago. (michael.casey@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:30 EDT - Buried in Tuesday's results from Candy Crush maker \uc2 King Digital Entertainment\uc2  (KING) was another reminder of why \uc2 Facebook\uc2  (FB) needs to move more strongly into the mobile payments market. The number of monthly active gamers fell 2% from the first quarter, which King attributed to a drop-off in people playing the game on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  on their desktop computers. \uc2 Facebook\uc2  earns 30% of revenues from transactions through its desktop payment platform -- but nothing from mobile, where developers use \uc2 Facebook\uc2  login tools for free. (lisa.fleisher@wsj.com, @lisafleisher)\par \par \uc2 12:30 EDT - Even as various surveys and anecdotal reports indicate homebuilders are using more sales incentives to boost volumes, the West Coast's \uc2 William Lyon Homes\uc2  (WLH) says its use of them remains relatively flat. Incentives--enticements such as free upgrades like premium flooring and financial assistance like helping buyers with closing costs--ranged from 0-4% of sales in 2Q and averaged 1.25%, roughly in line with previous results. But with WLH focusing on the higher end (2Q's average sales price surged 43% on-year to $500,500), that part of the market doesn't need incentives as much as entry-level buyers. While EPS beat consensus, shares are slightly lower today, putting the week's decline at 3.3%. (kris.hudson@wsj.com; @KrisHudsonWSJ)\par \par \uc2 12:26 EDT - \uc2 Barclays\uc2 ' new US stock-market strategist says buybacks are "on their way" to levels not seen since before the financial crisis. The bank estimates that companies use about 80% of their free cash flow after dividends to buy back their own shares. That's tacked on about 2% extra to EPS growth each year, firm says. "Now, there is nothing wrong with turning 2% revenue growth into 6% EPS growth," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says. But "we question if the market will pay peak multiples for engineered earnings." (alexandra.scaggs@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:24 EDT - A monthly survey of 150 sales managers by \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  finds demand for newly built homes fell both sequentially and from a year earlier in July, indicating the market continues to be muted amid limited consumer confidence and stringent mortgage-qualification standards. Of respondents, 25% reported better-than-expected July orders, down from 34% in June and 29% a year ago. Those saying orders missed estimates rose to 26% from 19% and 24%, respectively. Meanwhile, 21% reported increasing their use of sales incentives--free upgrades or financial assistance with closing costs--down from 25% in June but sharply higher than July 2013's 11%. (kris.hudson@wsj.com; @KrisHudsonWSJ)\par \par \uc2 12:20 EDT - Investors were already watching Brazil like a hawk before today's reports that Eduardo Campos, the candidate polling third in Brazil's October presidential election, has died in a plane crash. How markets react likely depends on what investors think this does to incumbent Dilma Rousseff's chances. Markets have been in 'anybody-but-Dilma' mode for months, a response to Brazil's sluggish growth and what some see as heavy-handed government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the economy. Investors bid up \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stock market as Dilma's poll numbers slid. Would Campos' death limit the chance that his party and Aceio Neves, No. 2 in the polls, will split the anti-Dilma vote? Brazil's stock index down 1.5% after holding near unchanged earlier. (matt.day@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:13 EDT - A who's who of big investors are doubling down on the deepwater Gulf of Mexico, pumping $1.3B of new equity into Warburg Pincus-backed oil explorer \uc2 Venari Resources\uc2 . Warburg was joined by fellow private-equity firms Kelso and The Jordan Co., BlackRock (BLK) and Singapore sovereign-wealth funds \uc2 Temasek\uc2  and GIC. The infusion follows $1.1B which launched Venari in 2012, since which the company--led by a former Nexen executive--has been involved in two huge oil discoveries (Shenandoah and Coronado) and become one of the largest leaseholders in the Gulf with 190 exploration blocks. (ryan.dezember@wsj.com; @RyanDezember)\par \par \uc2 12:12 EDT - Investors are still having a hard time discerning how much online sales are affecting retailing companies because of a hodgepodge of policies around what financial information retailers have to disclose about their ecommerce \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to \uc2 AlixPartners\uc2 . The consultancy said that one quick and dirty way to screen for retailers with better performing online businesses is to focus on those that disclose online sales in SEC filings. Revenue growth for firms like Nordstrom (JWN) that stated ecommerce results last year was 50% larger than non-disclosing companies, while disclosing companies' gross margins were 7% better than non-disclosing peers and their return on invested capital was 10% higher than companies that didn't report online sales, firm said. (shelly.banjo@wsj.com; @sbanjo)\par \par \uc2 12:10 EDT - Amazon (AMZN) is known to charge like a bull into any market it wants to conquer, losses be damned. That's what the e-retailer could be doing by charging an initial 1.75% fee to merchants who want to use its new Local Register credit-card payment system, says Baird. Many competing payment systems charge closer to 3%, with Square at 2.75% and \uc2 PayPal\uc2  Here at 2.7%. "The low rate suggests Amazon could be providing the new service at a loss," says Baird. AMZN's device will cost $10 while \uc2 PayPal\uc2  and Square give theirs away. AMZN is up 3.3% at $329.75 while \uc2 PayPal\uc2  parent \uc2 EBAY\uc2  falls 0.8%. (lisa.fleisher@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:09 EDT - While for years broadcast TV has borne the brunt of ratings declines, lately the pain has moved to cable. In July, broadcast's primetime ratings were down 3.7%, while cable networks were down 6.8%, according to Nielsen ratings covering the first three days after shows air, analyzed by MoffettNathanson analyst Michael Nathanson. "The last time cable underperformed broadcast by such a wide margin was during the London Summer Games" in 2012, he wrote. While the World Cup helped buoy broadcast somewhat, cable was truly a bloodbath, with \uc2 AMC Networks\uc2  the only cable network owner exempt from declines in total day ratings. (keach.hagey@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:00 EDT - Copper prices fell to their lowest level in more than seven weeks after a surprise decline in new lending has exacerbated concerns about the weakness of China's property sector, while a slowdown in industrial production underscored concerns that Beijing's targeted stimulus efforts are failing to boost growth. Copper is used in both manufacturing and construction, and China accounts for 40% of global demand. While the drop in lending could reflect the impact of government curbs on the Chinese property market and higher lending rates seen in recent months, "the extent of the collapse is alarming," \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  said. Comex Sep copper trades down 1.3% at $3.1140/lb. (tatyana.shumsky@wsj.com @MissShumsky) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com \par Cynthia Lin, 212 416-4403, Cynthia.lin@wsj.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181, john.shipman@wsj.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326, Patrick.sullivan@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 13, 2014 12:33 ET (16:33 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140813ea8d002rp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
