\par\par \b \uc2 The BOE's Sterling Talkdown Shows Freeloader Dilemma -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,295 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2014\par 18:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 12:33 EDT - The Bank of England's move to talk down the market's near-term rate hike expectations speaks to a kind of freeloader dilemma for policymakers: who would want to be the first \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates? The first one will suck in all the excess capital that's desperate for yield, driving up that country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and having a more exaggerated impact on competitiveness than would come from rate hikes in more "normal" times. The rest of the world gets to freeload. In that sense, Britain is the first major economy that can truly relate to the "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" problems that emerging markets such as Brazil would complain of a few years ago. (michael.casey@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:24 EDT - A monthly survey of 150 sales managers by \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  finds demand for newly built homes fell both sequentially and from a year earlier in July, indicating the market continues to be muted amid limited consumer confidence and stringent mortgage-qualification standards. Of respondents, 25% reported better-than-expected July orders, down from 34% in June and 29% a year ago. Those saying orders missed estimates rose to 26% from 19% and 24%, respectively. Meanwhile, 21% reported increasing their use of sales incentives--free upgrades or financial assistance with closing costs--down from 25% in June but sharply higher than July 2013's 11%. (kris.hudson@wsj.com; @KrisHudsonWSJ)\par \par \uc2 12:20 EDT - Investors were already watching Brazil like a hawk before today's reports that Eduardo Campos, the candidate polling third in Brazil's October presidential election, has died in a plane crash. How markets react likely depends on what investors think this does to incumbent Dilma Rousseff's chances. Markets have been in 'anybody-but-Dilma' mode for months, a response to Brazil's sluggish growth and what some see as heavy-handed government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the economy. Investors bid up \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stock market as Dilma's poll numbers slid. Would Campos' death limit the chance that his party and Aceio Neves, No. 2 in the polls, will split the anti-Dilma vote? Brazil's stock index down 1.5% after holding near unchanged earlier. (matt.day@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:14 EDT - Reports that Brazilian presidential candidate Eduardo Campos has died in a plane crash triggered volatility in the Brazilian stock market, with the benchmark Ibovespa index recently down 1.2% at 55753 points. Shares of \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 , which have been particularly sensitive to election-related news given the Brazilian government's heavy hand in company decisions, were off 2.5% at BRL19.17. (paul.kiernan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:00 EDT - The most-interesting thing in Markit's (MRKT) first quarterly report as a public company wasn't its results, but commentary from CEO Lance Uggla on the slower-than-expected adoption of electronic trading in over-the-counter products, especially derivatives. Moves to electronic trading will mean lower revenues for parts of the firm's business, but the assumption is baked into MRKT's business plan. Even though new regulations are in place for both buy and sell-side participants in the US, there has been no big change in trading behavior, with Uggla saying participants were using loopholes to avoid a move. For example, taking an ordinary rate swap and giving it a forward starting date makes it a structured derivative, which is exempt from the rules. MRKT is down 1.8% at $25.70. (paul.davies@wsj.com; @PaulJDavies)\par \par \uc2 "Canada Markets Trying to Guess What's in the Black Box -- Market Talk," published at 10:05 a.m. ET incorrectly identified the month of the labor survey. Statistics Canada found an error in the report for July, not August.\par \par \uc2 11:42 EDT - Southeast US businesses surveyed this month expect to see inflation rising slightly higher in the coming year then they did in July. The businesses anticipate 2.0% inflation in the next 12 months, up from 1.9% in July, according to \uc2 Atlanta Fed\uc2 's August business inflation expectations survey. About 47% of the businesses say their profit margins are at or above normal, a drop from 49% in July. The 211 firms surveyed -- many of them companies with national or international \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sales -- reported their unit costs were up, on average, 1.8% from August 2013, the same as the July survey. (cameron.mcwhirter@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:37 EDT - Markets appear to view the imminent arrival of a Russian aid convoy in eastern Ukraine as a de-escalation in tensions. But that sentiment is misplaced, says Nomura senior political analyst Alastair Newton. He thinks the apparent lack of proper coordination between Moscow, Kiev and the Red Cross runs the risk of the convoy being stopped at the border, resulting in a possible clash between Ukrainian and Russian forces. "I believe the Russian aid convoy, no matter how innocent in reality, runs the risk of triggering a dangerously volatile situation once it gets to the border. As such, it poses a potential threat to which markets should pay careful attention." Russia's MICEX index closed Wednesday up 1.8%. (andrea.tryphonides@wsj.com; @ATryphonides)\par \par \uc2 11:35 EDT - Both the \uc2 SPDR Barclays High Yield Bond ETF\uc2  (JNK) and \uc2 iShares iBoxx $ High Yield Corporate Bond ETF\uc2  (HYG) are back to trading at "premiums" to their net asset values. Share prices fell below their NAVs to a "discount" in recent weeks. Furthermore, both funds have seen inflows during the past two days, says Dave Lutz at JonesTrading, for the first time in six weeks. That flows and prices for these ETFs have stabilized is a case study for how transitory major rushes out the door can be. \uc2 RidgeWorth Investments\uc2 ' Alan Gayle told WSJ last month that he'd been selling some HY bond ETFs, but would be quick add to those positions if they fell low enough. (christopher.dieterich@wsj.com; @chrisdieterich)\par \par \uc2 11:29 EDT - Tepid consumer spending has raised questions over how robust US growth can be in 2H. Economists at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  cut their prediction for 3Q GDP by two-tenths to 3.1% after the report. The bank's GDP call for 2Q's revision remains at 3.9%. (min.zeng@wsj.com;@minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 11:05 EDT - A $24B sale of 10-year notes is due at 1 p.m. The to-be-auctioned notes recently yielded at 2.434%, the lowest since the June 2013 sale. Traders say the 14-month low yield may dent buying interest, though they say US bonds' superior yield compared to their counterparts in Germany and Japan likely lure buyers for relative value. Germany earlier Wednesday sold new 10-year bunds at a record low of 1.08%. (min.zeng@wsj.com;@minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 1459 GMT The Ukrainian hryvnia hit all-time lows this week and is on offer on the interbank market at 13.5 per dollar. \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says the hryvnia looks undervalued and its weakening does not appear justified by the country's fundamentals. Now its future depends on Russia's actions, particularly on President Vladimir Putin who is set to make a statement on Thursday. "Putin's statements in Crimea tomorrow should help clarify whether Russia will support a diplomatic resolution and de-escalation, or provide further support to the pro-Russia separatist movement in Ukraine," \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says. The bank forecasts the hryvnia to recover to 12.5 versus the dollar by the end of 2014.(andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 13, 2014 12:33 ET (16:33 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140813ea8d002ro\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
