\par\par \b \uc2 EXCLUSIVE-Brazil's Neves to allow  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  to float freely if elected\b0\par\par\uc2 599 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2014\par 20:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds political context, adviser's comments on impact of float)\par \par \uc2 By Jeferson Ribeiro\par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, Aug 7 (Reuters) - If Aecio Neves wins the presidency this October, he would allow Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float freely, leaving the market to set the exchange rate in a move that would likely help local industries struggling with a strong real.\par \par \uc2 Mansueto Almeida, a top economic adviser to the Neves campaign, told Reuters on Thursday that a free floating real will help restore balance to Brazil's external accounts and make Brazilian manufacturers more competitive.\par \par \uc2 "If elected, a Neves government will aim to end the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  populism imposed by President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2 's government and return to a free floating exchange rate," Almeida said in a written statement in response to questions from Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Almeida is a member of the team of advisers led by former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Arminio Fraga that are coordinating the economic policy platform for Neves, who is Rousseff's main challenger in the Oct. 5 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Since last August, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  daily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, derivatives designed to support the real. Many analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to avoid a sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation that would stoke already high inflation.\par \par \uc2 In June, the bank said it would extend the swap program until at least the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program has turned the real into the best-performing major Latin America \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, even with some recent losses. Some of those losses came after Neves told an audience of industry leaders on July 30 that Brazil needs a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make its economy more competitive in the global marketplace.\par \par \uc2 Industry and agribusiness leaders have called for a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make Brazilian exports more competitive and boost investment in a flagging economy. Critics have accused the Rousseff administration of using the exchange rate as a monetary policy tool to contain inflation.\par \par \uc2 The left-leaning Rousseff remains the favorite to win a second term, though recent opinion polls point to a close race that looks almost certain to go to a second-round runoff against Neves, a centrist from the Brazilian Social Democracy Party.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's economy has underperformed since Rousseff took office in 2011 despite repeated attempts to jump-start growth through tax cuts and other costly subsidies. Industrial output is set to shrink this year and remains below pre-2009 levels, hit by stubborn inflation and high labor costs.\par \par \uc2 Neves, a former state governor who has vowed to restore economic growth and investor confidence, hinted last week at what his exchange rate policy would look like.\par \par \uc2 "Brazil, unfortunately, is being held hostage to what we can call \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  populism, because the government seeks to control inflation through permanent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," Neves told an industrial lobby in Brasilia.\par \par \uc2 Almeida, an economist with the government think tank IPEA, said a free floating real would improve Brazil's trade balance and help reduce the current account deficit.\par \par \uc2 "The country's external accounts would enter a virtuous circle, attracting more investment and improving the trade balance," he said.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's current account deficit is running at close to 4 percent of gross domestic product and will hit $80 billion by year end, he added.\par \par \uc2 "That's a reflection of the imbalance caused by a real that has been artificially strengthened to contain inflation." (Writing by Anthony Boadle; Editing by Todd Benson and Meredith Mazzilli)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-8-10T18:11:25.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140807ea8700b19\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
