\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Central Bank \highlight \highlight22\uc2 FX Interventions \highlight\uc2  Could Lose Effect -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,409 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2014\par 15:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 09:55 EDT - The \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's actions to keep the real from losing value against the dollar, making the real a de facto managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , could be unsustainable, according to Sidnei Nehme, director of the NGO brokerage in Sao Paulo. The bank's policy of auctioning swap contracts, instead of using its large dollar reserves, exposes it to the risk of an abrupt change in the exchange rate, and keeping the real artificially strong hurts the Brazil's industrial sector, Nehme says. USD/BRL down 0.3% at 2.2155. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:52 EDT - \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  (MCD) big June SSS miss could have been due in part to the World Cup, some analysts speculate. "Anything that keeps folks at home watching TV keeps them away from their local \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 ," says Janney. SSS skidded 3.5% in the US and 3.4% in Europe. But soccer doesn't explain why MCD expects soft July sales as well. A complex menu and slow service likely are bigger factors. We'll learn more when MCD's conference call starts at 11. Shares are down 2.1% at $95.50. (julie.jargon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:49 EDT - Centene (CNC) is working to "manage the utilization" of new hepatitis C drugs. The health insurer estimates the drugs' costs in 2Q were consistent with its estimates at $13.7M, versus $4.2M a year ago and $7.3M in 1Q. The segment's drug prices gotten plenty of scrutiny of late, but CNC notes the growth also reflects membership gains. It is talking to states about reimbursing the expenses, the impact from which is now "plateauing" as the number of new people starting treatment is down and some people are winding up their stay on the drugs. States recognize the issue and "are being very constructive," says CEO Michael F. Neidorff. (anna.mathews@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:45 EDT - \uc2 DuPont\uc2  (DD) has identified further \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  it can trim away as part of the agricultural and chemical giant's "redesign," a multiyear effort it's pursuing alongside DD's planned separation of its performance-chemicals business, which makes house paints. CEO \uc2 Ellen Kullman\uc2  says during the 2Q conference call that DD is "questioning and understanding" all aspects of its operating model, which "hasn't been recast in a while." The company plans to spin off performance chemicals, a major contributor to revenue but prone to booms and busts, to focus on agriculture and other businesses where DD can command higher profit margins and more-reliable sales. Shares are down 0.6% at $65.15 as results were in line with last month's warning. (jacob.bunge@wsj.com; @jacobbunge)\par \par \uc2 9:42 EDT - With \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  (MSFT) Chief Executive \uc2 Satya Nadella\uc2  about six months into the job, some investors are growing antsy for him to commit MSFT to buying back more of its own stock or boosting other cash returns. MSFT repurchased about $6.1B of its shares in the nine months ended March 31, or a little more than one-quarter of the cash generated by its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  over that stretch. Wall Street believes Nadella can easily increase that percentage. MSFT reports results after the close of New York trading; analysts estimate revenue up 16% from a year earlier to $23B, with net income up 2% to 60 cents a share. MSFT gains 0.4% to $45. (shira.ovide@wsj.com; @shiraovide)\par \par \uc2 9:42 EDT - Centene (CNC) says that as of June 30, it had some 75,700 ACA-marketplace enrollees who had also paid premiums, more than the approximately 70K the health insurer had projected. That as demographics are "in line" with the projections embedded in CNC's pricing. It adds enrollees are mostly relatively low-income, with about 90% eligible for federal subsidies. Overall, CNC expects "minimal" financial impact from the exchanges this year; it's selling marketplace plans in 9 states. The company says it's too soon to provide details about its planned 2015 exchange pricing. Shares jump 5% to $79.40, hitting a fresh record, on its latest beat-and-raise. (anna.mathews@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:38 EDT - \uc2 United Technologies\uc2  (UTX) has a message for investors about a pair of engine failures which have marred the testing of its flagship military and commercial jet engines: nothing to see here. "These things do happen" in engine development, says CFO \uc2 Greg Hayes\uc2 , referring to the engine failure on a version of UTX's commercial geared turbofan engine and an engine fire which temporarily grounded the F-35 fighter jet. UTX's \uc2 Pratt & Whitney\uc2  makes both engines, and fixing the problems will be "relatively minor," says Hayes, adding engine development remains on schedule. UTX expects its first geared turbofan engines to enter service at the end of 2015. (ted.mann@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:36 EDT - What comes after the biggest job cuts in \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's (MSFT) history? When \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  reports earnings after the close of trading in New York today, the company for the first time will include a new segment with results from \uc2 Nokia\uc2  (NOK). The job cuts were about clearing the decks and creating a better cost structure, but Wall Street isn't sure new CEO \uc2 Satya Nadella\uc2  can both whack costs out of the \uc2 Nokia\uc2  business and at the same time lift the company's smartphone market share out of the sub-5% basement. (shira.ovide@wsj.com; @shiraovide)\par \par \uc2 09:32 EDT - \uc2 New York-Presbyterian Hospital\uc2  is renting "innovation space" at accelerator Blueprint Health, where the hospital will work with startups to more quickly develop new technologies that improve patient care as federal regulators have demanded, says NYP CIO Aurelia Boyer. She says the relationship with New York City-based Blueprint and the 50 health-care startups it currently hosts could lead to better health care technologies, some of which will benefit the hospital. "Having more dialogues as they're developing with us will get them a better product and...they'll develop around where we think we have needs," Boyer says. (clint.boulton@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:29 EDT - Hedge-fund luminaries like John Paulson and George Soros are liable to be handsomely rewarded from CIT announcing this morning its planned $3.4B acquisition of retail bank OneWest. For those who don't recall, OneWest was IndyMac, which collapsed in July 2008 ahead of the financial crisis' peak thanks to hisk-risk mortgages cut amid the housing bubble. At the time, it was one of the biggest US-bank failures and cost the FDIC's insurance fund some $13B. Afterward, the likes of Paulson, Soros and Michael Dell invested as some billionaires saw the opportunity--through IndyMac and other bank failures--to buy distressed assets which might be successfully turned around. IndyMac's assets were sold for $1.5B, and it bought a failed California lender a year, making OneWest the largest SoCal-based bank. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 09:22 EDT - \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  (\uc2 JPM\uc2 ) says it's launching a new $30M, 5-year initiative to support small business "clusters," or accelerators, that help growing small businesses in certain fields across 10 US cities. The new program, Small Business Forward, will connect entrepreneurs with resources and help certain accelerators provide participating small businesses with workforce and management training, partnerships with colleges and universities, and supplier networks, among others. The program, which includes small businesses in Detroit, Newark and New Orleans, could also help the largest US bank by assets gain customers, whether it's individuals or businesses. (emily.glazer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:22 EDT - Brazilian inflation remains high, sticky and generalized following the end of the World Cup while economic growth remains slow, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 ' Alberto Ramos. Mid-month inflation cooled from June and was less than expected, but the annual rate rose and is still above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 6.5% target ceiling. Ramos adds price increases are expected to accelerate further in August. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com \par Cynthia Lin, 212 416-4403, Cynthia.lin@wsj.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181, john.shipman@wsj.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326, Patrick.sullivan@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 22, 2014 09:55 ET (13:55 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140722ea7m00215\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
