\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Central Bank \highlight \highlight22\uc2 FX Interventions \highlight\uc2  Could Lose Effect -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,078 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2014\par 15:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 09:55 EDT - The \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's actions to keep the real from losing value against the dollar, making the real a de facto managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , could be unsustainable, according to Sidnei Nehme, director of the NGO brokerage in Sao Paulo. The bank's policy of auctioning swap contracts, instead of using its large dollar reserves, exposes it to the risk of an abrupt change in the exchange rate, and keeping the real artificially strong hurts the Brazil's industrial sector, Nehme says. USD/BRL down 0.3% at 2.2155. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1342 GMT [Dow Jones] Nomura closes out all its Russia positions as the framework has changed in light of the MH17 disaster, with more sanctions now likely. The investment bank had been operating on the assumption that a lack of imminent sanctions against Russia would allow investors to look at the fundamentals in Russia "where inflation has topped, domestic demand is weak and tight monetary policy was not justified." That has all changed in the wake of MH17, "and the probability of sanctions has significantly increased in our view." Nomura closes out its strategic trade in the local bond market and tactical trade in Russian credit. (matthew.cowley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1323 GMT [Dow Jones]--\uc2 Citigroup\uc2  sees a pick-up in G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading volumes, but the breakdown of the numbers only gives USD buyers a slight edge despite the greenback's general strength Tuesday. The bank flows favored USD buyers vs AUD, EUR, JPY and CHF, but USD sellers against NZD, GBP and CAD. (gary.stride@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:22 EDT - Brazilian inflation remains high, sticky and generalized following the end of the World Cup while economic growth remains slow, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 ' Alberto Ramos. Mid-month inflation cooled from June and was less than expected, but the annual rate rose and is still above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 6.5% target ceiling. Ramos adds price increases are expected to accelerate further in August. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:20 EDT - Home sales have disappointed analysts this spring but home prices still rose at a steady clip. An index maintained by the \uc2 Federal Housing Finance Agency\uc2  rose 0.4% in May, following a 0.1% increase in April, which was initially reported as no gain. For the year ended May, prices are up 5.5%, leaving them just 6.5% below their April 2007 peak. Still, the pace of increases has slowed in most of the US, with seven of nine Census regions reporting an annual gain that was lower than the one reported last May. (nick.timiraos@wsj.com; @NickTimiraos)\par \par \uc2 9:14 EDT - The National Association of Realtors will release data on June existing-home sales at 10, and watchers are waiting to see if May's results are repeated. The annual sales pace rose 4.9% from April, the initial print last month reported, generating a sigh of relief from many in the real-estate industry. The traditional spring selling season for homes got off to its worst start in years in March and April, so the question is whether May was just a one-month wonder. (joe.light@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1313 GMT [Dow Jones]--The ECB's balance sheet grew EUR5.4 billion to EUR2.06 trillion last week, according to the ECB's weekly report, somewhat bucking the recent trend of steady declines in its asset holdings. Still, the balance sheet is well below the EUR3 trillion-plus level from two years ago. The balance sheet should shrink again this week as banks repay a whopping EUR21.5 billion in three-year loans to the ECB. (brian.blackstone@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:09 EDT - Treasurys hit session highs as buyers continue to pile in. The core CPI dips back below 2% on an annual basis. Core inflation is expected to remain at or below the 2% mark for the remainder of the year, says Millan Mulraine, rate strategist at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 . Tame inflation "will continue to provide cover for [the Fed] to keep the policy rate on hold until Q4 2015, when we expect them to start tightening policy." Ten-year notes now 3/32 higher, yielding 2.462%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 9:06 EDT - Treasurys which guard investors from rising inflation are lagging their regular counterparts after the June CPI report showed little inflationary pressures outside of gasoline. Bond investors are paring their inflation expectations, which perked up after the May reading suggested pricing pressure was mounting. Three-year TIPS imply expectations for 1.77% average annual inflation the next 3 years, versus 1.82% before the report, while 10s reflect 2.21% expected inflation, from 2.23% beforehand. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 8:48 EDT - The tame core CPI reduces fears of an earlier Fed rate hike, encouraging investors to return to shorter-dated Treasurys. They have been under pressure lately as they're sensitive to changes in official rate-policy outlooks. Tame inflation also helps the long end recoup today's decline there, with the 10-year note now flat, yielding 2.476%. The 2-year is 1/32 higher, yielding 0.484%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 8:44 EDT - The dollar has added to its gains against the euro after June's CPI data were roughly in line with expectations, pushing the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its lowest level against the greenback since Nov. 21. It got down to $1.3459, versus a pre-data $1.3482, before rebounding somewhat, and is down 0.4% so far in the Americas session at $1.3472. (james.ramage@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1239 GMT The low level of government bond issuance in August in the euro zone is an argument for government bond yields to go lower next month, says \uc2 DZ Bank\uc2  analyst Christoph Kutt. August is traditionally a month of very low aggregate new issuance and redemption, which follows the "great spate" of maturities in July, so repaid cash will be looking for a new home, he says. These pressures were already perceptible last week. "A high demand, low supply situation is now emerging, with almost two thirds of the scheduled supply being sub-five-year maturities," he says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 22, 2014 09:55 ET (13:55 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140722ea7m001w2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
