\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Currency Intervention \highlight\uc2  Hurting Its Factories -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,568 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 2014\par 16:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 10:18 EDT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to prop up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight inflation are undercutting its factory sector, which is in a serious slump. Industrial production declines for the third straight month, according to figures out Wednesday. Part of the trouble is an overvalued real that hurts exports, says Alberto Ramos, chief Latin American economist at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . "They're using an improper set of instruments to deal with inflation," Ramos says. "The industrial sector is also being hurt by a significantly overvalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently extended daily \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap auctions to keep the real within a band of 2.20 to 2.25 per dollar to help tame inflation, which has been running near 6.5%. USD/BRL up 0.5% at 2.2133. (marla.dickerson@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:13 EDT - New York's top financial regulator Benjamin Lawsky says U.S. authorities are closer to finding the right way to deter wrongdoing at financial firms, citing \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 ' settlement over sanctions violations earlier this week. "The focus has to be on are we adequately deterring future bad conduct," Mr. Lawsky says on a radio show, noting there's been both criticism and praise of the BNP settlement. Aside from the record $8.9B fine that authorities handed to BNP, the bank also was forced to remove more than a dozen executives and faces restrictions on some business lines. The latter punishments represent Mr. Lawsky's push to impose penalties that have business consequences for financial firms that break the law. (andrewr.johnson@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:06 EDT - In an unusual Q&A interview to an internal newsletter published last evening, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Alexandre Tombini painted a benign scenario for inflation, but ended with a mixed message. Tombini said that, as "12-month inflation still shows resilience, monetary policy should stay vigilant to minimize risks," which seems to leave the door open for a new hike. But then he sort of pointed to the contrary, saying that "if current monetary conditions remain unchanged, inflation is trending towards a path that will converge" to the official 4.5% target. Inflation is now at 6.4%, while interest rates are at 11% after a year-long tightening cycle. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 10:05 EDT - The Canadian dollar could appreciate a bit more after its recent bout of strengthening, though investors are starting to find these levels attractive to short the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, says Charles St. Arnaud, executive director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research and economics at Nomura Securities. He says CAD's rise in recent weeks was mainly because investors who had been short were closing these positions as they come around to the view that the likelihood of a rate cut "is very close to 0" following stronger-than-expected inflation numbers. USD/CAD up 0.3% at 1.0661. (nirmala.menon@wsj.com; @NirmalaMenon)\par \par \uc2 1359 GMT [Dow Jones] European credit spreads might be set for a continued move tighter over the summer as bond supply dries up and new investor cash keeps pouring in. But \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  credit analyst Joseph M. Faith reckons the outlook is growing more complicated. Not only are investors running their highest exposure to credit in more than four years, according to a Citi survey, a pickup in U.S. growth could have wider implications on corporate funding costs as interest-rate expectations change. "While our central scenario remains bullish, we'd be looking to reduce our current tactical long should U.S. data continue to strengthen," he says. (ben.edwards@wsj.com @debtnerd)\par \par \uc2 1358 GMT [Dow Jones]--Hungary's plan to phase out foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans by end-2014 with banks paying hefty compensations to borrowers will be "pretty negative" for GDP, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . The refunds, especially if in cash, will boost consumption in 2H 2014 and early 2015 and CPI even beyond that. "A greater risk is that foreign banks will leave Hungary, and the sector will reduce its already limited willingness and/or ability to provide credit and fund a more sustainable investment recovery." As a result, Hungarian growth is likely to become even more dependent on external demand and fresh foreign direct investment inflows into its manufacturing industry. Exports will become even more crucial given Hungary's budgetary constraints. "With the country's public debt-to-GDP ratio still very high, at around 80% of GDP at end-1Q, the scope for countercyclical fiscal policy will be very limited in the years to come," BNPP says. (margit.feher@wsj.com @margitfeher)\par \par \uc2 09:58 EDT - US businesses with fewer than 50 employees added 117k jobs in June, the most since February 2012, according to ADP/\uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics. Firms with 50 to 499 employees added 115k jobs, and larger firms added just 49k. During a conference call, Mark Zandi, chief economist for \uc2 Moody's\uc2 , said small-business hiring has "kicked into gear" over the past two years, due largely to growth in housing-related construction, and that it's boosted consumer confidence because small firms employ the majority of US workers. But Washington, DC, economist Stephen Bronars cautions that the job market is still far from pre-recession levels. "This doesn't indicate a sea change," he says. "It's been a slow and steady recovery." (sarah.needleman@wsj.com; @saraheneedleman)\par \par \uc2 09:49 EDT - Wednesday's solid ADP report raises expectations for a strong June jobs report Thursday. The dollar is positioned to rise against its G10 brethren on a robust jobs number more than it is set to fall on a disappointing one. The reason: it's weakened so much against the pound, Aussie, yen and loonie of late that a lot of dollar weakness has been priced in, insulating it, to a degree, from further slides. Then again, "we're still in a low-volatility environment," says Robert Lynch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . "To the extent that it persists, the reaction of the dollar to the jobs data might not be as substantial as we expect." Dollar up 0.2% at Y101.77. Euro down 0.2% at $1.3650. (james.ramage@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:40 EDT - The much larger-than-expected 281K gain reported by ADP this morning raises optimism about tomorrow's nonfarm payrolls data. Median expectation from the WSJ survey is for a 215K gain. Stone & McCarthy is sticking to its 210K forecast, however, pointing out that initial ADP results have overshot the BLS report in the past four months. And since ADP revamped its calculation process in October 2012, ADP numbers have been off the BLS mark by an average 43K each month in either direction. One sector to watch is construction payrolls, which added just 6K jobs in May. ADP's June construction count rose a whopping 36K. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 09:39 EDT - Argentine bonds largely unchanged this morning after S&P late Tuesday put Argentina on watch for a potential downgrade. S&P currently rates Argentina at CCC-, which is three notches above default. The ratings firm said there's at least a one-in-two chance Argentina will default at the end of the month. The warning didn't put a dent in Argentine bond prices, which are trading at around 86.8 cents on the dollar from 85.9 cents Tuesday, although traders say volume is extremely light today. The next Argentina headlines may come Thursday, when the country's economy minister travels to Washington DC, to discuss the debt dispute at the \uc2 Organization of American States\uc2 . (nicole.hong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:24 EDT - Once again, gold traders are facing a conundrum. Charts indicate gold is positioned to go higher, and there's plenty of violence in Iraq and Ukraine to make a case for more gains. Yet traders are sitting on a 7% profit from June lows, and today's strong ADP report could be a harbinger of stronger-than-expected US jobs data Thursday, which would support the Fed raising rates sooner. The tough choices could explain why gold has shuffled between gains and losses all morning. "If gold can hold the $1,322 level today, the technicals will continue to indicate an upward bias," says Peter Hug, head of trading at Kitco Metals. For now, volume "is extremely thin out there." Gold at $1327.80 on Comex, up 0.1%. (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1316 GMT [Dow Jones]--\uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  has slashed its year-end forecast on 10-year Bund yields to 1.6%, from 2.25% previously. The firm has also cut its forecast for the end of 2015 to 2.25% from 3%. The changes are a result of the downward revisions to euro-zone inflation since last November (when the previous forecasts were published) and the ECB's easing measures taken in response, Goldman says. Even so, Goldman remains more bearish than forward markets imply. "Our switch from neutral to bearish on the direction of bond markets at the start of the second quarter was ill-timed. Looking ahead, however, we reaffirm our view that yields will rise above the forwards," the bank adds. (tommy.stubbington@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 02, 2014 10:18 ET (14:18 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140702ea72001zd\par }\page {\par