\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Currency Intervention \highlight\uc2  Hurting Its Factories -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,512 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 2014\par 16:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 10:18 EDT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to prop up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight inflation are undercutting its factory sector, which is in a serious slump. Industrial production declines for the third straight month, according to figures out Wednesday. Part of the trouble is an overvalued real that hurts exports, says Alberto Ramos, chief Latin American economist at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . "They're using an improper set of instruments to deal with inflation," Ramos says. "The industrial sector is also being hurt by a significantly overvalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently extended daily \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap auctions to keep the real within a band of 2.20 to 2.25 per dollar to help tame inflation, which has been running near 6.5%. USD/BRL up 0.5% at 2.2133. (marla.dickerson@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:14 EDT - Watch out \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2  (WMT), \uc2 Kroger\uc2  (KR) is looking a lot less like a neighborhood grocer these days as it morphs into a formidable competitor to take on the world's largest retailer. KR's latest $280M planned purchase of online vitamin store \uc2 Vitacost.com\uc2 , along with its recent $2.4B acquisition of \uc2 Harris Teeter\uc2 , will allow the Cincinnati grocery chain to push further into the heated online grocery wars, and go head-to-head with WMT and Amazon (AMZN). While WMT remains the biggest food retailer in the US, KR has been growing at a faster clip and posted nearly $100B in sales last year. KR off 0.6% at $49.23; WMT up 0.6% at $75.73. (shelly.banjo@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:13 EDT - New York's top financial regulator Benjamin Lawsky says U.S. authorities are closer to finding the right way to deter wrongdoing at financial firms, citing \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 ' settlement over sanctions violations earlier this week. "The focus has to be on are we adequately deterring future bad conduct," Mr. Lawsky says on a radio show, noting there's been both criticism and praise of the BNP settlement. Aside from the record $8.9B fine that authorities handed to BNP, the bank also was forced to remove more than a dozen executives and faces restrictions on some business lines. The latter punishments represent Mr. Lawsky's push to impose penalties that have business consequences for financial firms that break the law. (andrewr.johnson@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:06 EDT - In an unusual Q&A interview to an internal newsletter published last evening, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Alexandre Tombini painted a benign scenario for inflation, but ended with a mixed message. Tombini said that, as "12-month inflation still shows resilience, monetary policy should stay vigilant to minimize risks," which seems to leave the door open for a new hike. But then he sort of pointed to the contrary, saying that "if current monetary conditions remain unchanged, inflation is trending towards a path that will converge" to the official 4.5% target. Inflation is now at 6.4%, while interest rates are at 11% after a year-long tightening cycle. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 10:06 EDT - \uc2 Interpublic\uc2 's (IPG) strong new business results should continue to give a boost to the advertising company's top-line results, though new account wins may temporarily weigh on margins, RBC analysts say. IPG agencies have picked up some notable accounts this quarter, including \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's (MSFT) creative business. Though account wins are positive for ongoing business, RBC says IPG's margins could be choppy near-term due to the impact of hiring. Still, the firm says this is a 'high class problem' especially as IPG's management recently reiterated its full-year margin targets. RBC raises its price target to $21 from $19. IPG falls 0.5% to $19.38. (nathalie.tadena@wsj.com; @nftadena)\par \par \uc2 10:05 EDT - The Canadian dollar could appreciate a bit more after its recent bout of strengthening, though investors are starting to find these levels attractive to short the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, says Charles St. Arnaud, executive director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research and economics at Nomura Securities. He says CAD's rise in recent weeks was mainly because investors who had been short were closing these positions as they come around to the view that the likelihood of a rate cut "is very close to 0" following stronger-than-expected inflation numbers. USD/CAD up 0.3% at 1.0661. (nirmala.menon@wsj.com; @NirmalaMenon)\par \par \uc2 10:03 EDT - Many economists expect Bank of Canada to adjust wording of its next policy statement on July 16 to reflect the recent inflation's recent turn higher, and some suggest it could shift to a more hawkish position on rates, at least compared to its current neutral stance. Desjardins Capital Markets' economists believe BoC will stand pat, and say the paltry 0.1% GDP gain in April reported Monday will help justify that. "The Bank of Canada can be expected to leap at the opportunity of solidifying its neutral-dovish body of argumentation, especially as it will be refreshing its forecasts in two weeks," Dejardins says. (don.curren@wsj.com @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 09:58 EDT - US businesses with fewer than 50 employees added 117k jobs in June, the most since February 2012, according to ADP/\uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics. Firms with 50 to 499 employees added 115k jobs, and larger firms added just 49k. During a conference call, Mark Zandi, chief economist for \uc2 Moody's\uc2 , said small-business hiring has "kicked into gear" over the past two years, due largely to growth in housing-related construction, and that it's boosted consumer confidence because small firms employ the majority of US workers. But Washington, DC, economist Stephen Bronars cautions that the job market is still far from pre-recession levels. "This doesn't indicate a sea change," he says. "It's been a slow and steady recovery." (sarah.needleman@wsj.com; @saraheneedleman)\par \par \uc2 9:56 EDT - \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  (\uc2 JPM\uc2 ) shares down 1% at $57.00 in morning trading, underperforming the S&P 500's flattish start, after the bank's disclosure that CEO \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  has been diagnosed with throat cancer. Fifteen minutes into the session, enough shares had traded to account for 19% of the stock's daily average volume, according to FactSet. But considering Dimon's stature at the bank, that's "shockingly" little activity, said RJ Grant, associate director of equity trading at financials-focused brokerage KBW. "The way they phrased it -- highly treatable and all that -- I think people are willing to take it at face value." (matthew.jarzemsky@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:51 EDT - Fissures within the Republican party over the future of the \uc2 U.S. Export-Import Bank\uc2  continue to widen. Three South Carolina Republicans -- Gov. Nikki Haley, and Sens. Lindsey Graham and Tim Scott -- sent a letter to congressional leaders on June 27, calling for the bank's expiring charter to be reauthorized. They wrote that the agency helps promote and finance U.S. exports. \uc2 General Electric\uc2  (GE) has a large presence in South Carolina, and is also a large beneficiary of the Ex-Im Bank. (damian.paletta@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:49 EDT - Wednesday's solid ADP report raises expectations for a strong June jobs report Thursday. The dollar is positioned to rise against its G10 brethren on a robust jobs number more than it is set to fall on a disappointing one. The reason: it's weakened so much against the pound, Aussie, yen and loonie of late that a lot of dollar weakness has been priced in, insulating it, to a degree, from further slides. Then again, "we're still in a low-volatility environment," says Robert Lynch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . "To the extent that it persists, the reaction of the dollar to the jobs data might not be as substantial as we expect." Dollar up 0.2% at Y101.77. Euro down 0.2% at $1.3650. (james.ramage@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:40 EDT - The much larger-than-expected 281K gain reported by ADP this morning raises optimism about tomorrow's nonfarm payrolls data. Median expectation from the WSJ survey is for a 215K gain. Stone & McCarthy is sticking to its 210K forecast, however, pointing out that initial ADP results have overshot the BLS report in the past four months. And since ADP revamped its calculation process in October 2012, ADP numbers have been off the BLS mark by an average 43K each month in either direction. One sector to watch is construction payrolls, which added just 6K jobs in May. ADP's June construction count rose a whopping 36K. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com \par Cynthia Lin, 212 416-4403, Cynthia.lin@wsj.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181, john.shipman@wsj.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326, Patrick.sullivan@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 02, 2014 10:18 ET (14:18 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140702ea720021d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
