\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Currency Intervention \highlight\uc2  Hurting Its Factories -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 156 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 2014\par 16:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 10:18 EDT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to prop up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight inflation are undercutting its factory sector, which is in a serious slump. Industrial production declines for the third straight month, according to figures out Wednesday. Part of the trouble is an overvalued real that hurts exports, says Alberto Ramos, chief Latin American economist at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . "They're using an improper set of instruments to deal with inflation," Ramos says. "The industrial sector is also being hurt by a significantly overvalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently extended daily \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap auctions to keep the real within a band of 2.20 to 2.25 per dollar to help tame inflation, which has been running near 6.5%. USD/BRL up 0.5% at 2.2133. (marla.dickerson@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 02, 2014 10:18 ET (14:18 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140702ea720021c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
