\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Vale buoys Brazil Bovespa; real gains past 2.2/dlr\b0\par\par\uc2 693 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2014\par 23:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Updates with real closing stronger than 2.2/dlr)\par     By Walter Brandimarte\par     RIO DE JANEIRO, June 26 (Reuters) - A jump in shares of\par mining company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vale SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  helped keep Brazil's benchmark Bovespa\par index in the black on Thursday, while the Brazilian real closed\par stronger than 2.2 per dollar for the first time in more than two\par months.\par     Vale's preferred shares climbed 2.2 percent as\par futures for iron ore, the company's main export product, jumped\par nearly 4 percent in China. Traders were also\par encouraged by a Reuters report that the mining firm has started\par to offer discounts on shipments of the raw material to China.\par  \par     "The market is betting on a possible increase in Vale's\par market share," said Luis Gustavo Pereira, an analyst with Guide\par Investimentos in S\u227\'00\'E3o Paulo.\par     Vale's preferred and ordinary shares accounted for most of\par the 0.15 percent gain recorded by Brazil's benchmark Bovespa\par index. The index had posted losses over the past\par sessions on concern about the future of state-run oil company\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petroleo Brasileiro SA\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     Shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petrobras\f28 \uc2 \f2 , as the oil firm is known,\par rose 0.2 percent but were unable to fully recover from a plunge\par of more than 5 percent during the past two sessions following a\par government announcement that it would sell the company oil\par exploration rights to four fields off Brazil's southeast coast. \par     Analysts say investments in those oil fields will further\par weigh on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petrobras\f28 \uc2 \f2 ' already stretched financial capacity.\par     Also weighing on Brazil's market sentiment was the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's quarterly inflation report that showed policymakers\par expect the country to grow less this year, while inflation\par remains resilient. \par     \par     REAL GAINS PAST 2.2/DLR\par     Meanwhile, the Brazilian real  erased early\par losses and closed past the psychologically relevant level of 2.2\par per dollar for the first time since early April. It ended at\par 2.1953, 0.4 percent stronger on the day.\par     The real had already rallied 1 percent on Wednesday after\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announced its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  program\par would remain intact through the end of the year, providing ample\par hedge for investors concerned about possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  losses.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  program, which includes daily auctions\par of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swaps, has been one of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's most\par successful initiatives to stabilize the country's volatile\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     It has helped the real trade largely between 2.20 and 2.25\par per dollar during the past few months - a tight range that is\par considered a sweet spot both for exporters and policymakers\par worried about inflation from imported goods.\par     Dollar inflows at the end of the session eventually pushed\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  past the level of 2.2 on Thursday, forcing some\par investors to unwind long dollar positions and boosting the real\par even further.\par     "As soon as you see a move like that, investors start to\par unwind a lot of trading positions," said the manager of the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  desk with a large Brazilian bank.\par    \par  \par     \par     Key Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and stock indexes at 2040 GMT:\par     \par  Stock indexes                   daily %    YTD %\par                                   change   change\par                       Latest              \par  MSCI Emerging          1046.81     0.48      4.4\par  Markets                                  \par  MSCI LatAm             3373.98    -0.13     5.41\par                                           \par  Brazil Bovespa        53506.75     0.15     3.88\par                                           \par  Mexico \f28 \uc2 \f2 IPC\f28 \uc2 \f2              42613.8    -0.56    -0.27\par                                           \par  Chile IPSA             3886.91    -0.18     5.07\par                                           \par  Chile IGPA               18954    -0.19     3.99\par                                           \par  Argentina MerVal        7820.5    -2.03    45.07\par                                           \par  Colombia IGBC         14180.99    -0.23     8.49\par                                           \par  Peru IGRA             16553.74     0.36     5.08\par                                           \par  Venezuela IBC          2131.44    -0.93   -22.11\par                                           \par                                                  \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                       daily %    YTD %\par                                   change   change\par                          Latest           \par  Brazil real             2.1953     0.42     7.36\par                                           \par  Mexico peso            13.0138    -0.08     0.12\par                                