\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil Bovespa hits 7-month high, real adds to gains\b0\par\par\uc2 566 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2014\par 20:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par RIO DE JANEIRO, June 10 (Reuters) - Brazilian stocks and\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  climbed on Tuesday, with the benchmark Bovespa index\par reaching its highest level in over seven months, on speculation\par President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dilma Rousseff\f28 \uc2 \f2  will lose more ground in upcoming\par election polls.\par     Other Latin American markets were slightly down as investors\par started pricing in prospects of higher U.S. rates, which cut the\par appeal of higher-yielding emerging market assets.\par  \par     Brazil's Bovespa index rose for a third consecutive\par session, climbing as much as 0.5 percent to 54,549.50, its\par highest intraday level since Oct. 30, 2013.\par     Traders said investors were betting that upcoming opinion\par polls would show growing chances of a second round in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par October presidential elections. Rousseff, whose \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionist\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par policies have been highly criticized by investors, remains a\par favorite to win the vote but her lead has fallen in recent\par polls.\par     Contributing the most to gains on the Brazilian bourse were\par shares of state-run companies such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco do Brasil\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petroleo Brasileiro SA\f28 \uc2 \f2 , or \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petrobras\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which are\par expected to benefit from less government meddling in their\par businesses.\par     Shares in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco do Brasil\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the nation's largest bank by\par assets, jumped 2.7 percent while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petrobras\f28 \uc2 \f2 ' stock rose 0.7\par percent.\par     Other Latin American bourses were in the red as U.S. 10-year\par yields hit a one-month peak, sapping investor appetite in\par emerging market assets in general. Mexico's \f28 \uc2 \f2 IPC\f28 \uc2 \f2  stock index\par  dropping 0.2 percent, while MSCI's index for Latin\par America was practically unchanged.\par     In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, the Brazilian real  edged\par 0.15 percent higher to 2.2261 per dollar, keeping gains that had\par been fueled by a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announcement that it would extend\par its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  program past June 30.\par     Mexico's peso, on the other hand, weakened 0.1\par percent to 13.0475 per dollar. The Mexican \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been\par slowly sliding since the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unexpectedly cut\par interest rates on Friday. \par     Chile's peso led losses in Latin America, dropping\par 0.8 percent to 554.00 per dollar. The move came on the back of a\par sharp sell-off in the price of copper, the country's main\par export, which was hit by concern about the impact of an\par investigation into metal financing in China.    \par       \par   \par     \par     Key Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 1815 GMT:\par     \par  Stock indexes                  daily %    YTD %\par                                  change   change\par                      Latest              \par  MSCI Emerging         1056,67     0,74     4,61\par  Markets                                 \par  MSCI LatAm             3394,3     0,04        6\par                                          \par  Brazil Bovespa       54376,28     0,19     5,57\par                                          \par  Mexico \f28 \uc2 \f2 IPC\f28 \uc2 \f2            42923,97     -0,2     0,46\par                                          \par  Chile IPSA            3907,97     -0,1     5,64\par                                          \par  Chile IGPA           19086,96    -0,08     4,72\par                                          \par  Argentina MerVal      8314,76     0,27    54,23\par                                          \par  Colombia IGBC        14080,26      1,2     7,72\par                                          \par  Peru IGRA            16051,64     0,01     1,89\par                                          \par  Venezuela IBC         2208,17     3,49   -19,31\par                                          \par                                                 \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                      daily %    YTD %\par                                  change   change\par                         Latest           \par  Brazil real            2,2261     0,15     5,87\par                                          \par  Mexico peso           13,0475    -0,13    -0,13\par                                          \par  Chile peso                554    -0,76    -5,04\par                                          \par  Colombia peso         1882,82     0,01     2,61\par                                          \par  Peru sol                2,792    -0,11     0,04\par                                          \par  Argentina peso         8,1275     0,03   -20,12\par  (interbank)                             \par                                          \par  Argentina peso          11,66    -0,17   -14,24\par  (parallel)                              \par                                          \par  \par  (Reporting by Walter Brandimarte; Editing by Bernadette Baum)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-6-10T19:36:14.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140610ea6a00bo6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100