\par\par \b \uc2 Can Brazlian Real Continue To Gain? -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shuli Ren  \par \uc2 350 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 2014\par 19:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Investors who bought the \uc2 iShares MSCI Brazil Capped ETF\uc2  (EWZ) in early February would have collected over 20% returns by now, in part because of the 10+% rally from the Bovespa equity index, but also thanks to a stronger Brazilian real.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  broadly rallied since early February. Indonesian rupiah gained 5.5%. South African rand advanced 6.2%. Turkish lira was up 6.7%.\par \par \uc2 But Brazilian real has done particularly well, advancing 7.8% since. Why is it?\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has continued to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the swap markets to support the real and it is still raising interest rates to combat inflation. But just like the stocks, the real may well have received a boost from President Dilma Rousseff's falling approval rating. Capital Economics analyst Neil Shearing explains:\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have received a boost from the latest opinion polls, which suggest that support for President Rouseff is weakening and have led to speculation that a more market-friendly and less \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  government might emerge from October's elections.\par \par \uc2 Can the real rise further? Capital Economics is skeptical:\par \par \uc2 Brazil is especially vulnerable if Chinese demand for industrial commodities weakens further, as we expect.\par \par \uc2 Any expectation that October's elections will herald a change in government and a shift in policy seems premature -- Ms. Rousseff is still the overwhelming favourite. Meanwhile, the economic outlook remains weak and we fear that new risks are likely to emerge over the next 6-12 months. In particular, the continued rapid expansion of credit in recent years seems to be storing up problems for the future.\par \par \uc2 More fundamentally, while the real does not appear as expensive as it once did, it still looks overvalued on several measures. The current account deficit remains large -- the 12 month rolling sum is still more than $80bn, just shy of 4% of GDP. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  More at Barron's Emerging Markets blog,  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/ }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April 30, 2014 13:28 ET (17:28 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140430ea4u003r4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
