\par\par \b \uc2 Central Banker Tombini Says Brazil Well-Prepared for QE Pullback -- WSJ Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pedro Nicolaci da Costa   \par \uc2 306 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2014\par 19:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Brazil has built up enough domestic buffers against rapid shifts in capital flows to allow it to withstand a pullback from unconventional interest rate policy in the world's largest economies, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Alexandre Tombini said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Tombini agreed in part with Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Raguram Rajan, who argued during a \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2  speech that aggressive monetary easing in advanced economies had made life harder for developing countries.\par \par \uc2 But he was less optimistic than Mr. Rajan about the likelihood of cooperation between monetary authorities of different countries.\par \par \uc2 "I'm skeptical that you can have too [much] international coordination" on monetary policy, Mr. Tombini said.\par \par \uc2 He was also much less concerned than Mr. Rajan about the possibility that a pullback in Federal Reserve bond buying and an eventual increase in interest rates would be strongly damaging to emerging market growth.\par \par \uc2 "We've been able to ride these global financial cycles in different ways. During the expansionary phase we take our measures and try to build up our [foreign reserve] buffers or recourse to multilateral liquidity facilities," Mr. Tombini said. "This buffer is now being handy during the exiting or normalization phase."\par \par \uc2 In particular, Mr. Tombini said that by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  was able to "implement a relatively independent policy from the rest of the world."\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian economy expanded only by 2.3% last year and is forecast to grow even less in 2014. Despite this weakness, high inflation has forced the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to repeatedly raise interest rates, which this month were lifted to 11%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at The Wall Street Journal's Real Time Economics blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/economics/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/economics/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April 10, 2014 13:21 ET (17:21 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140410ea4a002se\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
