\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's Real Closes Stronger After  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paulo Trevisani   \par \uc2 465 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 2014\par 23:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA--The Brazilian real strengthened against the dollar Friday amid signs that the government may not be willing to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as some traders expected.\par \par \uc2 The real exited active trading at BRL2.2435 per dollar, stronger than BRL2.2826 at closing the day before, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\par \par \uc2 The last time the real traded at the BRL2.24 level was in early November.\par \par \uc2 The appreciation comes one day after \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Investors Services said it will likely keep Brazil's stable rating outlook throughout the year. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  has an investment-grade rating on Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Last month, \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  rating agency downgraded Brazil to the lowest investment grade.\par \par \uc2 High interest rates in Brazil may also be attracting foreign investors who are lured by returns that are higher than in developed markets, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 But the main force behind Friday's performance may have been the fact that \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't support the dollar as some had expected.\par \par \uc2 For months now the bank has been following a schedule of daily auctions of contracts meant to provide investors with an alternative to reduce their exposure to the Brazilian real. In these contracts, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bets on the real strengthening in the future, while allowing investors to do the opposite.\par \par \uc2 As the contracts expire, the bank has the option to roll them over, thus strengthening the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the real has accumulated gains against the dollars in the last few weeks, after bottoming out at BRL2.44 in January. That performance made investors believe that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would roll over just a small amount of the derivative contracts, effectively weakening the real--something that could help Brazilian companies to better compete with foreign rivals. But the rollover came higher than many expected.\par \par \uc2 "The large rollover was the main factor behind the real today," said Paulo Petrassi, a partner at investment firm Leme Investimentos, in Sao Paulo. He said he thinks it a sign that the government likes the real strong because it can potentially curb inflation, even though Brazil's fundamentals may not support the appreciation.\par \par \uc2 "There is a lot of speculative capital flowing in," Mr. Petrassi said, "but economic fundamentals are very bad."\par \par \uc2 The analyst said low growth and high inflation expectations for 2014 are among the factors why the real should be weaker.\par \par \uc2 "I think the real's current level is its ceiling," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Petrassi said he had expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to end the year at BRL2.50, but is now thinking of changing that to BRL2.45.\par \par \uc2 Write to Paulo Trevisani at paulo.trevisani@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 04, 2014 17:11 ET (21:11 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140404ea44003pd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
