\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil stocks drop on profit-taking; Latam  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  up\b0\par\par\uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2014\par 19:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SAO PAULO, April 1 (Reuters) - Brazil's benchmark Bovespa\par index led losses in Latin American equity markets on Tuesday,\par after closing out March with its biggest rise in more than two\par years, while most \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the region strengthened on\par continued dollar inflows.  \par     The Bovespa index fell 0.8 percent as investors took\par some profits after the index rose 7 percent in March. Mexico's\par IPC index dipped 0.1 percent following a 4.3 percent rise\par in the previous month.  \par     The declines came even as expectations rose that China will\par act to support its economy following weak manufacturing data for\par March and that the U.S. Federal Reserve will keep its monetary\par stimulus for longer after dovish comments from Chair Janet\par Yellen on Monday. \par     "Overseas markets continue with a positive tone after\par Yellen's remarks, but we were very stretched here," said Luiz\par Roberto Monteiro, an equities trader with brokerage Renascen\u231\'00\'E7a\par in S\u227\'00\'E3o Paulo.\par     As of Monday, the Bovespa had recorded gains in 10 of the\par previous 11 sessions, closing above 50,000 points for the first\par time in nearly three months.\par     Among the biggest decliners in the Brazilian market were\par shares of telecommunications firm Grupo \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oi SA\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which\par slid 3 percent after sources said banks were considering pulling\par out of a 6 billion real ($2.7 billion) share offering by the\par company. \par     Shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ita\u250\'00\'FA Unibanco Holding SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 0.2\par percent as the bank's weighting on the Bovespa index was poised\par to rise in the May-August period, according to the first preview\par of the index released by the bourse. \par     Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  extended their recent rally, with\par traders saying dollars continued to flow into Latin America as\par investors sought higher returns.\par     Brazil's real rose 0.5 percent to 2.26 per dollar,\par though gains were curbed by uncertainty about the pace of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rolled over only part of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par swaps that expired on Tuesday in a decision that was seen by\par analysts as a sign that policymakers do not want the real to\par strengthen much past the level of 2.3 per dollar.\par     \par  \par     \par     Key Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 1643 GMT:\par     \par     \par  Stock indexes                   daily %    YTD %\par                                   change   change\par                       Latest              \par  MSCI Emerging          1000.22     0.56     -0.8\par  Markets                                  \par  MSCI LatAm             3184.34    -0.31    -0.21\par                                           \par  Brazil Bovespa        50018.62    -0.79    -2.89\par                                           \par  Mexico IPC            40413.62    -0.12    -5.41\par                                           \par  Chile IPSA             3788.38     0.41     2.41\par                                           \par  Chile IGPA            18608.63      0.3     2.09\par                                   