\par\par \b \uc2 Brazilian Bonds Make a Comeback\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicole Hong and Matt Wirz   \par \uc2 970 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 21:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 U.S. fund managers are snapping up Brazilian debt, reviving a market that last year was among the biggest casualties of emerging-markets turmoil.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian government bonds denominated in the real, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , have returned 3% to investors this year, the third-best performance, behind Indonesia and Thailand, among the 15 countries tracked on a \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  index of emerging-markets local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt. The share of Brazil's local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds held by foreigners jumped to a four-year high of 16.5% in January, according to \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 Investors say they are attracted to real-denominated government and corporate bonds because of their high yields and relatively low risk when compared with debt issued by other developing nations. Brazil's benchmark interest rate, the rate underlying trading in all other debt in the market, is 10.75%, compared with 3.5% in Mexico and 12% in Nigeria, according to data from \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Brazil is the clearest case of mispricing in emerging markets," said Bryan Carter, a portfolio manager at Acadian Asset Management, whose firm oversees $400 million in fixed-income assets. Mr. Carter said he recently boosted his Brazilian bondholdings to 13% of his portfolio, the biggest bullish position he has.\par \par \uc2 The rally highlights how investors' desire for income-generating investments is driving demand for riskier bonds at a time when safe assets offer meager returns. Brazil's 10% bonds due in 2021 are yielding 12.12%, compared with 2.3% on similar \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  bonds.\par \par \uc2 Appetite for new bonds from yield-hungry investors is allowing troubled borrowers to raise new debt. That contrasts with previous emerging-markets crises, when bond markets shut their doors to companies and countries in the developing world.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, Brazilian oil company \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  received $22 billion of orders for an $8.5 billion bond sale.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian yields are high in part because the Brazilian real's 15% depreciation against the dollar last year and the country's 6% annual inflation pushed down bond prices. Yields rise when prices fall.\par \par \uc2 But some investors say the high yields overstate the threat to bond prices posed by inflation and rising interest rates.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year started a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program to stabilize the real, which has appreciated 2.2% this year against the dollar and outperformed most of its emerging-market peers. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has raised interest rates at eight consecutive meetings and is widely expected to raise them again at its next meeting on April 2.\par \par \uc2 "The notion of this crisis in Brazil has abated," said Gorky Urquieta, co-head of emerging-market debt at \uc2 Neuberger Berman\uc2 , which manages $242 billion and has added to its Brazilian bondholdings this year. "It's a healthier environment."\par \par \uc2 Some bargain hunters also are snapping up bonds of Brazilian corporations that were hit by a flurry of high-profile bankruptcy filings, such as oil company OGX and sugar and ethanol producer \uc2 Aralco\uc2  SA A\u231\'00\'E7\u250\'00\'FAcar & \u193\'00\'C1lcool. The filings prompted investors holding bonds of other Brazilian companies to sell, pushing prices down and yields up. That created bargains in the bonds of competitors such as Tonon Bionergia SA, says Robert Abad, who manages a $53 billion emerging-markets bond fund for \uc2 Legg Mason\uc2 's \uc2 Western Asset Management Co\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 To be sure, much uncertainty remains. Parts of Brazil are experiencing their worst drought in decades, which could lead to water shortages and subsequent price increases that will keep inflation high in the short run. Also, economic growth is expected to stay slow, as Brazil's economy nearly slipped into recession last year. Other challenges include Brazil's plans to host the FIFA World Cup in June and hold national elections in October, a sequence that some observers say could discourage needed government-spending cuts.\par \par \uc2 Two funds run by \uc2 Pacific Investment Management Co\uc2 ., one of the world's biggest money managers, sold about $2.9 billion of Brazilian government bonds in the last three months of 2013, according to calculations based on Pimco data. Pimco, a unit of \uc2 Allianz SE\uc2  of Germany, didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  Ratings Services cut its credit rating on Brazil to BBB-, the first sovereign downgrade for Brazil since 2002. S&P cited Brazil's slowing growth and large fiscal deficit, putting the country one notch away from junk territory.\par \par \uc2 While the move was largely anticipated by the markets, analysts at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  say the downgrade could make it more difficult for Brazilian companies to raise money. S&P, along with Moody's Investors Service and \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 , all have Brazil on a stable outlook, meaning another downgrade isn't imminent.\par \par \uc2 Other bulls on Brazil are buying stocks, not bonds. Mark Mobius, executive chairman of \uc2 Templeton Emerging Markets\uc2  Group, said in an interview Monday that he is especially bullish on Brazilian consumer stocks as rising wages in Brazil will lead to more domestic consumption--a trend that could hit bondholders, whose investments tend to do better in slow-growth environments.\par \par \uc2 Even so, some investors say they are happy to stay in Brazil for now since they feel they are adequately compensated for all the risks.\par \par \uc2 "Is Brazil an economy that can really go off a cliff tomorrow? No. And yet, it's paying you yields that are very compelling," said Javier Murcio, a portfolio manager at Standish Mellon Asset Management Co., which manages about $160 billion and has added to its Brazilian bondholdings this year.\par \par \uc2 Min Zeng contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Nicole Hong at nicole.hong@wsj.com and Matt Wirz at matthieu.wirz@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 25, 2014 16:48 ET (20:48 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140325ea3p003hy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
