\par\par \b \uc2 *Brazil's Real Closes Sharply Weaker on 'Perfect Storm'\b0\par\par\uc2 720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2014\par 19:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 27 Jan 2014 13:19 EDT *Brazil's Real Exits Active Trading at BRL2.4214 Per US Dollar\par \par \uc2 27 Jan 2014 13:20 EDT *Brazil's Real Closes at Weakest Level Since Aug. 22, 2013\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 This item was corrected at 1:52 p.m. ET because it misstated the date of the real's weakest level.\par \par \uc2 27 Jan 2014 13:51 EDT  CORRECT: Brazil's Real Closes at Weakest Level Since Aug. 22, 2013\par \par \uc2 27 Jan 2014 14:01 EDT  Brazil's Real Closes Sharply Weaker on Emerging-Market Worries\par \par \uc2 By Jeff Fick\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO--Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed sharply weaker against the U.S. dollar Monday as political and economic turmoil in developing economies around the world sent investors scurrying for safety.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real exited active trading at BRL2.4214 to the dollar, 0.9% weaker from Friday's closing price fix of BRL2.3993, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet. Monday's close was the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakest since BRL2.4334 on Aug. 22, the day before the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program that has continued.\par \par \uc2 Concerns about growth in emerging-market powerhouse China and rising expectations that the U.S. Federal Reserve will further reduce stimulus measures are at the heart of the current sell-off, traders said. But images of protests in Ukraine and Thailand, as well as political and economic turmoil in Argentina and Turkey, have touched a nerve with investors and darkened the already gloomy outlook for the developing world.\par \par \uc2 "Markets are very nervous," said Win Thin, global head of emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2 , calling the confluence of recent events a "perfect storm" for emerging markets. But the recent unease has its roots in late May 2013, when the first mention of an end to the Fed's easy-money policies first emerged, Mr. Thin said. "Ever since the chatter with tapering started, emerging markets haven't been able to gain any traction," Mr. Thin said.\par \par \uc2 The Fed is expected to announce another $10 billion reduction in its bond-buying program that would put it at $65 billion per month, following a similar move in December. The reduced stimulus has caused interest rates in the U.S. to rise ever so slightly, especially for 10-year Treasurys, making the paper more attractive than such emerging markets as Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Despite recent declines, Brazil's real has actually held up relatively well compared with Argentina and Turkey. Argentina's peso tumbled nearly 20% on Thursday before the government eased restrictions on dollar purchases, while Turkey's lira also tumbled to its weakest level ever against the greenback ahead of an emergency meeting of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set for Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "If emerging markets are selling off, Brazil will sell off as well," Mr. Thin said. "But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan is keeping \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  from being a big underperformer."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil\uc2  has so far avoided abrupt swings by supplying the market with dollars via \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap contracts sold in daily auctions that were first introduced last August. The bank sold about $1.43 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap contracts during Monday's session, including the roll over of some contracts coming due in February.\par \par \uc2 The sale of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps typically supports the real against the U.S. dollar by allowing investors to exchange bonds indexed to domestic interest rates for paper tied directly to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The real will likely continue to weaken in the near term as the issues in other developing countries exacerbate Brazil's homegrown problems with weak growth, high inflation and a widening fiscal deficit, traders said.\par \par \uc2 "All of this negative information is forcing multinational companies to buy dollars as a hedge against a speculative attack on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Marcos Trabbold, chief of trading \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Sao Paulo's B&T Associados. Mr. Trabbold said that the real could reach BRL2.50 to the dollar unless the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  increases the amount of swaps sold or reintroduces the sale of dollar credit lines that ended in late December.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jeff Fick at Jeff.Fick@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 27, 2014 14:01 ET (19:01 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140127ea1r002qc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
