\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Real steadies, Mexico peso drops for second day\b0\par\par\uc2 404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2014\par 17:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par RIO DE JANEIRO, Jan 22 (Reuters) - The Brazilian real\par steadied on Wednesday following a drop in the previous session,\par but the Mexican peso weakened for a second day as investors\par remained cautious about a possible reduction in U.S. monetary\par stimulus later this month.\par     Speculation that the Fed was poised to cut its monthly bond\par purchases by another $10 billion at the end of its Jan. 28-29\par policy meeting caused Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slump in the\par previous session, with the Brazilian real tumbling 1 percent.\par     "Yesterday's sell-off shows the market is very sensitive (to\par the Fed)," said Reginaldo Siaca, a manager at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  desk\par of Advanced brokerage in Sao Paulo. "Today investors are on the\par sidelines, awaiting more news."\par     * The Brazilian real  was little changed at\par 2.3614 per dollar, also supported by strong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. \par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Wednesday sold 4,000 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par swaps, derivatives that provide protection against a weaker\par real, as part of its daily \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  program. It also sold\par 25,000 additional swaps to roll over similar contracts that\par mature early next month. \par     * The Mexican peso dropped 0.3 percent, however, in\par its second consecutive session of losses. It has lost more than\par 2 percent so far this year as investors who were bullish on the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cut back on long peso positions as domestic data\par disappointed.\par     * The Chilean peso gained 0.5 percent, partly\par recovering from six consecutive sessions of losses that caused\par it to shed 3 percent of its value.\par     * Chile's peso has been pressured by falling prices of\par copper, the country's main export product, and expectations that\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may cut domestic interest rates in coming\par months, further reducing the allure of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \par \par     Most traded Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 1535 GMT\par     \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                       daily %   YTD %\par                                   change  change\par                         Latest            \par  Brazil real            2.3614     -0.06   -0.19\par                                           \par  Mexico peso           13.3140     -0.29   -2.13\par                                           \par  Chile peso           541.4000      0.54   -2.83\par                                           \par  Colombia peso       1979.2000      0.32   -2.38\par                                           \par  Peru sol               2.8070      0.04   -0.50\par                                           \par  Argentina peso         6.9100     -0.36   -6.04\par  (interbank)                              \par                                           \par  Argentina peso        11.9200     -0.59  -16.11\par  (parallel)                               \par                                        