\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Changes  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Plan; Real Weakens -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,288 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Dezember 2013\par 15:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 09:01 EST - In the wake of the Fed's taper decision, the \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also modified its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, planning to scale back the use of dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps aimed at strengthening the real. "The size of the BCB 'taper' is a signal that it believes the peak of the pressure on BRL has passed, and is so taking a bullish stance on the effects of the Fed's actions on" the real, said Tony Volpon of Nomura. Seems like the announcement itself is hurting the real though, down another 1.3% against the dollar today, at BRL2.3578. Nomura expects BRL to weaken to the BRL2.45-per-dollar area in 2014. (prabha.natarajan@wsj.com; @prabhanatarajan)\par \par \uc2 8:44 EST - A second straight week of discouraging layoff indications, with jobless claims back at their highest since March. The fact that the Fed has decided to taper will continue to put pressure on Treasurys, but economic data need to back up that decision. Treasury prices tick up from session lows after the release. Ten-year notes down 9/32 to yield 2.92% and 30-year bonds up 14/32 to yield 3.892%. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 1341 GMT [Dow Jones]--Since the fall of Bretton Woods in the early '70s, the dollar has followed a long-term cycle with up- and down- trends lasting on average seven years; seven fat, seven lean. Very biblical. Even though the 2008 financial crisis extended the downtrend that had started in 2002, says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 , especially against emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , "..we remain comfortable with the long-term cycle view of the dollar." Indeed, the dollar trade-weighted index appears to have troughed in 2011. So this would mark the downtrend as lasting nine years, longer than the 1970s downtrend, but shorter than the 1990s version. From here, it could be a long way up. (david.cottle@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:41 EST - Thought the dollar was bulletproof after the long-awaited taper? Not quite. A surprise jump in weekly jobless claims knocks the greenback into negative territory for the day against the yen, reminding us that markets will continue to keep a close eye on data to gauge how long the wind-down of Fed stimulus is going to take. Do expect the market to be somewhat more forgiving of bad data, however, now that the Fed has started trimming its stimulus. The dollar is down 0.1% at Y104.17. (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:36 EST - After last week's surprise jump, claims unexpectedly keep rising. At 379,000 last week, claims are at the highest since March. This covers the employment survey week, but the problems with seasonal factors this time of year make month-to-month comparison harder to do. Even so, rising claims don't shout the "labor-market improvement" that the Fed is counting on seeing when it announced tapering yesterday. (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 08:26 EST - Colombia's GDP growth in 2Q surprised to the upside and 3Q data due out this morning might do the same as this oil-driven economy rebounds from an short-lived slowdown in late 2012 and early 2013. Analysts forecast an on-year 3Q expansion rate of 4.3% as domestic demand continues to grow due to a bigger middle class and rising tax revenue that has allowed the government to ramp up infrastructure spending. The country's finance minister says the GDP rate may be even higher than forecasts, so analysts say they won't be shocked if the print is 5% or more. dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:25 EST - Investors overnight continued to react to the Fed's decision to buy fewer bonds starting in January. As expected, middle-dated notes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been a big buyer of are seeing the most selling pressure. Adding to that is a $29B of 7-year notes to be sold at 1pm. That maturity is down 14/32 to yield 2.316% while 30s are the only gainers, up 7/32 to yield 3.904%. The long bond is the most sensitive to the threat of inflation, which is fading as the Fed pulls back. 10s are down 11/32, yielding 2.927%, the highest since mid-September. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 1257 GMT [Dow Jones]--The shekel continues to weaken against a U.S. dollar stronger more or less across the board after the Fed's taper announcement Wednesday. Traders say the market may have a more local flavor next week, and through to month end, as many foreign banks and investors will be absent for Christmas and New Year holidays. At 1237GMT USD/ILS was up 0.16 at 3.5106. (saratoth@gmail.com)\par \par \uc2 1237 GMT [Dow Jones]--Despite the notion that global investors shifted portfolio allocations away from EM \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Mr. Bernanke's first 'taper scare' on May 22, BBH says price action would suggests otherwise. While the USD has had mixed fortunes elsewhere, against EM it has largely gained as tapering expectations picked up during 4Q. The bank looks for this trend to continue in the 1Q of 2014, with the so-called 'fragile five,' BRL,IDR,INR,TRY and ZAR, likely to continue underpeforming. USD/TRY trades at a fresh 3-month high of 2.0677, USD/IDR a 5-year high 12238.0. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:30 EST - As we know when tapering will begin, one of the next questions is how long will it take for QE3 to disappear. Will it be mid-2014, which seems to have been the guidepost many have been pointing to, or will Yellen take things slower as she assumes the leadership mantle? There's much to sort as as next year gets going. Meanwhile, on tap this morning economics-wise beyond jobless claims is November reads on leading indicators and existing-home sales and December's Philly Fed index, all at 10. S&P 500 futures are down a half-point as gold slides 2.5% amid a second-thought about tapering's impact. Nymex oil is flat, as is the euro at $1.3685, while Treasurys weaken. The 10-year yield is at 2.91%. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1146 GMT [Dow Jones] Euro Overnight Index Average fixed lower Wednesday at 0.176% versus Tuesday's 0.206%. Thursday, the interbank overnight rate is trading in a narrower range between 0.10% and 0.15% vs 0.10% and 0.20% Wednesday. The amount banks deposited overnight at the ECB increased only slightly to EUR 44 billion from EUR 43 billion. Earlier Thursday, 3-month Euribor fixed at 0.293% vs 0.269% Wednesday. (nicky.redl@dowjones.com) Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 1143 GMT [Dow Jones] For all the recent talk of a "Great Deceleration", Capital Economics reckons that the bulk of the Emerging Market growth slowdown is already behind us, and that growth (in aggregate) looks set to be broadly unchanged in the New Year from this one. However, the house says, this prognosis masks important differences between individual countries and regions, and there are a number of economies where consensus still looks too optimistic. "The BRICs are likely to remain weak by recent standards, but growth in a number of smaller EMs should pick up - Nigeria, central Europe - Poland in particular - and oil-producing Gulf nations," Capital says.(david.cottle@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 19, 2013 09:01 ET (14:01 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131219e9cj0020c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
