\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Changes  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Plan; Real Weakens -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,374 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Dezember 2013\par 15:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 09:01 EST - In the wake of the Fed's taper decision, the \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also modified its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, planning to scale back the use of dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps aimed at strengthening the real. "The size of the BCB 'taper' is a signal that it believes the peak of the pressure on BRL has passed, and is so taking a bullish stance on the effects of the Fed's actions on" the real, said Tony Volpon of Nomura. Seems like the announcement itself is hurting the real though, down another 1.3% against the dollar today, at BRL2.3578. Nomura expects BRL to weaken to the BRL2.45-per-dollar area in 2014. (prabha.natarajan@wsj.com; @prabhanatarajan)\par \par \uc2 8:56 EST - Semtech (SMTC) shares drop after the chip maker cut its fiscal 4Q guidance, blaming delays to carrier capital spending and the ramp up of a new consumer mobile platform. B Riley cuts recommendation to sell from neutral saying "a third consecutive outlook miss casts doubt on the strength of SMTC's end markets." Sterne Agee sticks to its long-term buy, calling the slowdown in the communications segment "temporary" with "secular growth drivers" intact and Raymond James expects shares to outperform following the sharp selloff. SMTC shares, which had traded over $37 in June, drop 17% to $23.25 premarket. (patrick.sullivan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:44 EST - A second straight week of discouraging layoff indications, with jobless claims back at their highest since March. The fact that the Fed has decided to taper will continue to put pressure on Treasurys, but economic data need to back up that decision. Treasury prices tick up from session lows after the release. Ten-year notes down 9/32 to yield 2.92% and 30-year bonds up 14/32 to yield 3.892%. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 8:41 EST - Thought the dollar was bulletproof after the long-awaited taper? Not quite. A surprise jump in weekly jobless claims knocks the greenback into negative territory for the day against the yen, reminding us that markets will continue to keep a close eye on data to gauge how long the wind-down of Fed stimulus is going to take. Do expect the market to be somewhat more forgiving of bad data, however, now that the Fed has started trimming its stimulus. The dollar is down 0.1% at Y104.17. (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:39 EST - Arguably one of the most-disappointing quarterly report from a homebuilder this year as KB's (KBH) F4Q earnings rose no where near as much as analysts anticipated while revenue increased 7%, half of Street projections. That as closings fell 4%, a very-disappointing result at a time where homebuilders have continued to see strong growth in that metric this year while working through their backlogs. There's no comment in KBH's statement about why closings fell on-year on the West Coast last quarter, something which will surely come up during the 11:30 conference call. But CEO Jeffrey Mezger does note KBH's average price in California soared 29% in 4Q. Shares fall 2.3% premarket at $17.15 after trading sideways since mid-year as as last quarter's orders were flat. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:36 EST - After last week's surprise jump, claims unexpectedly keep rising. At 379,000 last week, claims are at the highest since March. This covers the employment survey week, but the problems with seasonal factors this time of year make month-to-month comparison harder to do. Even so, rising claims don't shout the "labor-market improvement" that the Fed is counting on seeing when it announced tapering yesterday. (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 08:26 EST - Colombia's GDP growth in 2Q surprised to the upside and 3Q data due out this morning might do the same as this oil-driven economy rebounds from an short-lived slowdown in late 2012 and early 2013. Analysts forecast an on-year 3Q expansion rate of 4.3% as domestic demand continues to grow due to a bigger middle class and rising tax revenue that has allowed the government to ramp up infrastructure spending. The country's finance minister says the GDP rate may be even higher than forecasts, so analysts say they won't be shocked if the print is 5% or more. dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:25 EST - Investors overnight continued to react to the Fed's decision to buy fewer bonds starting in January. As expected, middle-dated notes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been a big buyer of are seeing the most selling pressure. Adding to that is a $29B of 7-year notes to be sold at 1pm. That maturity is down 14/32 to yield 2.316% while 30s are the only gainers, up 7/32 to yield 3.904%. The long bond is the most sensitive to the threat of inflation, which is fading as the Fed pulls back. 10s are down 11/32, yielding 2.927%, the highest since mid-September. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 8:22 EST - After drilling for 35K feet, you hope to have something to show for it. But Cobalt (CIE) says an exploration well in the Gulf of Mexico "did not encounter commercial hydrocarbons" in the targeted zones, and so the wellbore will be capped and abandoned. CIE has a 60% stake in the effort, with Total (TOT) holding the rest. CIE, which doesn't yet generate revenue, has been drilling off Angola and Gabon as well as the Gulf. Today's news comes 4 months after shares slid 15% after the prospector said a Gulf well off Louisiana didn't hit anything. They trended still lower through Thanksgiving, afterward CIE slumped anew on results from Angola. Shares are down 4.2% premarket at $15 after having already dropped 30% this month. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:10 EST - Rite Aid (RAD) 3Q earnings match expectations, though drugstore operator cuts its full-year earnings outlook expecting meager same-store sales growth of 0.35%-0.85%. RAD shares drop 5% premarket to $5.45, but that is likely due as much to the 324% YTD gain as any true disappointment with the results. For 3Q, front-end same-store-sales slipped 0.2% and pharmacy sales were hit by new generic drug launches. (patrick.sullivan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:08 EST - A week after entering into exclusive talks, Essex (ESS) has agreed to acquire fellow West Coast apartment company BRE for $4.34B under the nonbinding terms made public then. The deal will create an entity owning some 56K unites and 239 properties in California and Seattle while ESS intends to keep its annual dividend at current levels after the deal closes. BRE has been weighing its options for more than a year, and ESS has long been seen as the likeliest buyer if BRE were to choose that route. BRE, whose shareholders are getting some ESS stock in the deal, will initially own a 37% stake in the bulked-up ESS. Both are inactive premarket. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:43 EST - \uc2 Coca-Cola Enterprises\uc2  (CCE) gives a downbeat initial 2014 profit forecast, anticipating a below-long-term-target growth of 10%. That as "we expect underlying challenges to continue and impact growth in 2014," says CEO \uc2 John Brock\uc2 . Meanwhile, sales are seen rising by a low-single-digit consensus, under consensus views of a 5% gain, according to Thomson Reuters. The European bottler does hopes for a demand boost amid the coming World Cup soccer tournament in Brazil. CCE, inactive premarket, will today give back some of its 33% stock gain for 2013. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December 19, 2013 09:01 ET (14:01 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131219e9cj001xo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
