\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Prepared to Deal With Easing of Stimulus, Says Rousseff\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paulo Trevisani   \par \uc2 740 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Dezember 2013\par 22:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA--Brazil has reacted correctly during times of global crisis and is ready to deal with potential turbulence coming from the expected easing of stimulus measures in developed economies during 2014, the country's president said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 President Dilma Rousseff added that her administration aims to improve her country's business environment and maintained her staunch defense of much-criticized Finance Minister Guido Mantega.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Rousseff said during an unusually long talk with reporters at the presidential palace that she hopes for an economic recovery in the U.S. and Europe and sustained growth in China, Brazil's three biggest trading partners.\par \par \uc2 The president spoke for almost two hours at a breakfast with reporters. The event marked the end of a year that saw criticism of her administration's handling of the economy, but also saw the president launch an ambitious program to overhaul key parts of the country's infrastructure with private-sector capital.\par \par \uc2 In the past few years, a number of changes, often temporary, in the tax code, combined with regulatory shifts have sparked criticism that uncertainty could be reducing Brazil's attractiveness for investment.\par \par \uc2 "You can't create a new tax one year, take it out in the next; make safety rules and step back just before they kick in," said Ilan Goldfajn, chief economist at S\u227\'00\'E3o Paulo-based \uc2 Itau Unibanco\uc2  bank, in an interview. "All this creates uncertainties that cause investments to be postponed."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Rousseff defended her administration's actions, saying they were designed to counter the economic downturn that has seen three consecutive years of growth below initial government forecasts.\par \par \uc2 "All governments interfere with the economy during crises," Ms. Rousseff said, adding \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts are "costly and the sooner they are removed, the better."\par \par \uc2 Brazil's gross domestic product expanded 7.5%, a historic high, in 2010, the year Ms. Rousseff was elected. GDP growth then slowed to 2.7% in 2011 and bottomed out at 1% in 2012. The economy is expected to grow less than 2.5% this year and at about the same pace in 2014.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Rousseff cited the bond-buying program by the U.S. Federal Reserve as an example of other governments' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Fed's policy strengthened \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's and other emerging-markets' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , making their exports less competitive and causing some of them, including Brazil, to implement capital controls.\par \par \uc2 The outlook for the Brazilian economy going into 2014 is uncertain. There could be trouble in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as developed economies are expected to resume growth and unwind stimulus measures. That could weaken the Brazilian real and other emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Even with relatively slow growth, inflation in Brazil is expected to remain near the top of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target range of 2.5% to 6.5%. The 12-month reading now stands at 5.8%.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently raised its benchmark interest rate to 10% to try to slow the pace of price increases.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "knows how to make the transition" to less stimulus spending in developed economies, Ms. Rousseff said.\par \par \uc2 Some observers agree Brazil is ready for tapering.\par \par \uc2 "Brazil is better positioned than many other emerging markets...to withstand the change in global liquidity that tapering would imply," said Marcela Meirelles, a Latin America analyst with fund-managing firm TCW.\par \par \uc2 "Brazil suffers from a weak growth outlook...and some fiscal metrics have deteriorated," she said. "But if you look at the long-risk indications, it is far from being vulnerable to balance of payment crises caused by a change in global liquidity."\par \par \uc2 The president, who is expected to run for re-election next year, is also preparing to reshuffle her cabinet as some ministers will have to step down to be able to run for public office.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Rousseff didn't provide any details of the possible plans but did say that Finance Minister Guido Mantega isn't expected to leave his post.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mantega has played an important role in Brazil's economic policy for the last decade and is set to become the country's longest-serving finance minister. Yet he has also faced harsh criticism for the slow growth.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mantega "is perfectly [fine] where he is," Ms. Rousseff said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Paulo Trevisani at paulo.trevisani@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 18, 2013 16:19 ET (21:19 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131218e9ci003re\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
