\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  dip on Fed anxiety; real gains\b0\par\par\uc2 444 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2013\par 20:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, Dec 17 (Reuters) - Most Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dipped in thin trading volumes on Tuesday as investors remained on the sidelines before a crucial decision by the U.S. Federal Reserve on whether to cut down on stimulus on Wednesday. Although most economists do not expect the Fed to start reducing the pace of its bond purchases until next year, many are expecting U.S. policymakers to communicate more clearly that such a move is imminent. A reduction in the Fed's bond-buying program - which currently injects $85 billion a month in the U.S. economy - could reduce capital inflows into emerging market economies and sap the strength of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The market will likely remain volatile until tomorrow, with thin volumes," said Sidnei Nehme, a director at NGO brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2  "Investors will likely remain on the sidelines until the end of the Fed meeting." * Brazil's real gained 0.3 percent, bucking a weakening trend seen in Latin American peer \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . * \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday sold 10,000 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps that are part of its daily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, designed to provide investors with protection against a possible depreciation of the real. The bank also sold an additional 20,000 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps to roll over similar contracts that mature in the beginning of 2014. * \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to announce "adjustments" to its swap sale program this week and analysts believe policymakers may increase the pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary to offset any negative market impact from a possible Fed tapering. * Mexico's peso weakened 0.2 percent as investors continued to pocket recent gains triggered by last week's congressional decision to open the country's oil and gas sector to private investment. * Chile's peso closed steady at 527.30 per dollar after three consecutive sessions of gains. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trimmed early gains after failing to cross its 30-day moving average of 524.70 per dollar. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 1921 GMT:\par     \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                         daily %    YTD %\par                                     change   change\par                            Latest           \par  Brazil real               2.3203     0.31   -12.08\par                                             \par  Mexico peso              12.9595    -0.22    -0.74\par                                             \par  Chile peso              527.3000    -0.02    -9.22\par                                             \par  Colombia peso          1942.0000    -0.41    -9.06\par                                             \par  Peru sol                  2.7700    -0.47    -7.91\par                                             \par  Argentina peso            6.3300    -0.24   -22.39\par  (interbank)                                \par                                             \par  Argentina peso            9.7200    -0.21   -30.25\par  