\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Real May Face More Depreciation in 2014 -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,503 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2013\par 17:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:05 EST - The Brazilian real will face more depreciation in 2014, but the process will be "smoothed out" by continued \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to Banco Itau chief economist Ilan Goldfajn. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  is backed up by considerable foreign reserves," he says. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will maintain its policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." The real has depreciated by about 15% so far in 2013. Higher US interest rates next year could force additional depreciation. Real last trading at BRL2.34 per dollar. Itau is forecasting BRL2.35 for the end of this year and a gradual depreciation to BRL2.45 by the end of 2014. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:58 EST - Capital Economics expects the housing recovery to down shift in 2014 to a "more sustainable path," as the strong level of purchases by investors and tight supply conditions moderate. "The next stage of the recovery will be characterised by strengthening activity among owner-occupiers and mortgage-dependent buyers, a rising number of willing sellers and a much more moderate pace of house price inflation." The firm notes valuation and affordability are turning less favorable, but "the overall picture is still that housing is on the cheap side." Higher prices will increase the number of willing sellers, though Capital Econ expects price gains to slow to around 4% a year in 2014 after climbing about 11% this year. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 10:55 EST - Itau is forecasting a 2014 base rate for the Selic of 10.25%, just a quarter-point above where \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the rate to last month. "Recent signals indicate the cycle is coming to an end," says Itau chief economist Ilan Goldfajn, a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy governor. He adds a nearly completed hiking cycle would imply policy makers are willing to tolerate continued inflation. Goldfajn sees 2014 inflation little changed from 2013 at about 6%. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:53 EST - Colombia's government began talking peace in late 2012 for the first time in a decade, and after 13 months of negotiations in Cuba with Marxist rebels, one can confidently state the peace process has done nothing to stem guerrilla violence aimed at the oil sector. The Colombian Oil Association, a voice for multinational companies operating here, said today the 151 rebel attacks on oil pipelines in 2012 have already been surpassed this year, with 163 such bombings through late November. The attacks have cut 30k barrels a day off Colombian output, which averages 1M bpd. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:34 EST - A $21B sale of 10-year notes at 1pm EST will provide signals about how bond investors are handling the much-talked about QE taper. R.W. Pressprich's Larry Milstein is optimistic about the sale, arguing that the bond market is now better prepared for tapering with the 10-year yield up from 1.6% at the start of May. "We have seen buying since [Friday's] employment report, which would indicate that investors anticipate any taper to be very slow and gradual," he says. Earlier selling that sent yields higher could make the sale relatively more attractive to some buyers, such as Japan investors who have been scooping up bonds in recent weeks, traders say. Ten-year notes down 8/32, yielding 2.828%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 10:30 EST - US bond mutual funds have watched more than $70B go out the door so far this year, according to TrimTabs. And with still two weeks left to go in 2013, outflows have already topped the previous yearly record of $62.5B set in 1994 -- when the Fed raised rates and caused the one major hiccup in the bond market's three-decade bull run. The next two weeks won't breeze by either: the Fed delivers a policy statement on Dec. 18, one that could reduce the pace of stimulus and undoubtedly further dent investors' appetite for bonds. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 10:28 EST - Regarding late Tuesday's trumpeted US budget deal, \uc2 Pierpont Securities\uc2  chief economist Stephen Stanley says, "It is a mistake to view this as an important substantive achievement." He notes the agreement will modestly increase the deficit versus prior spending plans the next 2-3 while doing nothing to address long-term fiscal problems. "If the success here could reasonably be expected to lead to a bigger deal down the road, then we might want to get more optimistic." But for now, Stanley argues, "This deal was only made possible because all of the tough issues were taken off the table at the offset." (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 1523 GMT [Dow Jones]--Takarekbank raises its forecasts for Hungary's GDP growth to 1.2% for this year, from 0.7%, and to 2.2% for next year, from 1.7%, on forecasting a rise in domestic demand and an increase in investment. Economist Gergely Suppan sees a strong possibility that Hungary may embark on a course of steady growth. The question is whether that will be sustainable and if it keeps accelerating, he adds. Large external debt will continue to act as a drag, thus export performance will need to remain key for Hungary, he says. Expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut the policy rate next week to 3.00% from the current 3.20%. That should be the bottom for the current easing cycle since a main rate lower than that would raise financial stability concerns, Mr. Suppan adds. (margit.feher@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:44 EST - The \uc2 Chicago Fed\uc2 's National Financial Conditions Index (NFCI) provides a comprehensive weekly update of US financial conditions and the index's latest data print suggests financial conditions remain looser than average. The reading of -0.94 in the week ended December 6 has the index holding steady for the third week in a row, printing its lowest level since February 1994. Looser-than-average financial conditions would be one factor in the Federal Reserve's decision to taper monetary policy earlier rather than later. (vincent.cignarella@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 9:43 EST - \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says benign inflation in Peru gives the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  space to provide stronger monetary stimulus, predicting 2 more quarter-point rate cuts are forthcoming. The investment bank also thinks policy makers will need to let the sol depreciate to rebalance Peru's current account. "We forecast the USD/PEN to depreciate to PEN2.90 in 2014"; it finished Tuesday at PEN2.793 and is down 9.4% this year. \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  made a quarter-point interest-rate cut last month, the first move in 2 1/2 years. The next meeting is Thursday. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:42 EST - The Stock Trader's Almanac notes how copper tends to turn bullish in December, having a tendency to make a major seasonal bottom this month and peaking in April or May. If that's the case then one should expect a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Chilean peso to find its footing around the same time. USD/CLP is trading well-above all its moving averages, with the peso down 7.5% against the dollar since October 17, pretty much in a straight line. CLP has recently completed wave 5 in the current wave cycle and a retrace is highly likely. A target to aim for would be the 100-day moving average of CLP511.24, roughly where it was trading when the \uc2 Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut rates, sending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into a spin. (vincent.cignarella@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1442 GMT [Dow Jones]--Ukraine's main economic problem is the overvalued hryvnia, says Vladimir Osakovsky, CIS chief economist at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  in Moscow. Selling pressure on the hryvnia has increased recently after social unrest hit some of Ukraine's major cities in late November. The \uc2 National Bank of Ukraine\uc2  targets the hryvnia and spends money to support its rate, which nearly halved its coffers over the past two years to $18.8 billion as of late November. The relatively strong hryvnia pressures exports, boosts imports and erodes companies' incomes, Mr. Osakovsky says. If Ukraine strikes a deal on financial aid with the IMF, the hryvnia could ease by some 15%, while in case of an uncontrolled devaluation it could lose some 20%-25% to UAH10-10.5 per dollar, Mr. Osakovsky says. This could help to reduce the current account deficit. A sovereign default looks unlikely for now and could only happen if reserves shrink further and Kiev doesn't agree on aid from either the IMF or Russia. (andrey.ostroukh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 11, 2013 11:05 ET (16:05 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131211e9cb0028q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
