\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Weaker Early Ahead Of Rate Meeting\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gerald Jeffris   \par \uc2 322 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2013\par 13:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA--Brazil's real weakened in early trading Wednesday as investors looked toward a moderate tightening of local interest rates by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a monetary policy meeting later in the day.\par \par \uc2 As of 6:50 a.m. EST, the real traded at BRL2.3015 to the dollar after ending at BRL2.2936 to the dollar Tuesday, according to Tullett Prebon via Factset.\par \par \uc2 Traders noted the real was weaker amid expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would hold off on a forceful rate hike to help preserve the country's economic growth. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen raising the country's benchmark Selic rate by half a percentage point at its meeting to 10% as part of an effort to hold local inflation below 6%.\par \par \uc2 The pace of economic growth has been a concern in Brazil as the country looks toward possible disappointing third-quarter figures to be released next week.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen maintaining \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local exchange market to help provide dollar liquidity and maintain the real's strength.\par \par \uc2 The bank has scheduled to roll over 22,000 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  contracts Wednesday afternoon on part of a maturity of some $10 billion in swaps coming due next week.\par \par \uc2 The rollover will come in addition to some $500 million in swaps scheduled for sale at a regular daily auction.\par \par \uc2 The swap contracts allow investors to exchange bonds indexed to domestic interest rates for paper tied to the U.S. dollar and typically lead to a strengthening of the real.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this year amid concerns over investor risk aversion and capital outflows brought by talk of policy changes at the U.S. Federal Reserve.\par \par \uc2 Write to Gerald Jeffris at gerald.jeffris@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 27, 2013 07:04 ET (12:04 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131127e9br001a9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
