\par\par \b \uc2 Brazilian Real Slides as Holiday Amplifies Fed Speculation\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paulo Winterstein   \par \uc2 473 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2013\par 20:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--Brazil's real weakened against the dollar Monday as the prospect of reduced U.S. stimulus measures following last week's strong jobs data continued to weigh on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the same time that a holiday curbed trading volume and exaggerated losses.\par \par \uc2 The real exited active trading at 2.3335 per dollar, after closing at BRL2.3111 to the dollar Friday.\par \par \uc2 Job creation in the U.S. surprised on the upside last week, with the Labor Department saying that U.S. payrolls expanded by 204,000 jobs in October, almost double the 120,000 new openings expected. The data led to increased bets that the Federal Reserve could start tapering its bond-buying program as soon as December. The bond-buying program, by making it cheaper to borrow dollars, has helped lift emerging-markets assets and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and a reversal of such a program is expected to rapidly weaken those assets.\par \par \uc2 After the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slumped against the dollar Friday, it did so once more Monday, a move exacerbated by the fact that a U.S. holiday drastically reduced trading volume.\par \par \uc2 "Today orders are lighter than when you have inflows from foreigners, so you're going to have more volatility," said Italo Abucater, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at \uc2 ICAP Plc\uc2  in Sao Paulo. "Add to that the fact that you have more dollar-buying because of really awful flows into the country in recent months."\par \par \uc2 From the start of the year through Nov. 1, a net of almost $5.4 billion had left the country, compared with net inflows of $18 billion at a comparable time last year. With international companies operating in Brazil traditionally sending home profits and dividends at the end of the year, that outflow will likely increase in coming months, as happened at the end of last year.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market as part of its stated effort to reduce volatility. The bank sold about $500 million in foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap contracts Monday and promised to start rolling over part of the $10 billion in contracts that expire next month.\par \par \uc2 The bank rolled over about two-thirds of contracts that expired Nov. 1 but waited until the end of the month to start rolling them over.\par \par \uc2 On Friday the bank announced it would start rolling over some contracts already, and that created some relief for investors, according to Mr. Abucater. The bank likely wanted to make clear it would roll over more contracts--which have the practical effect of strengthening the real against the dollar--after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped 6% over the past month, Mr. Abucater said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Paulo Winterstein at paulo.winterstein@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 11, 2013 14:03 ET (19:03 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131111e9bb002hp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
