\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Ends Weaker on Inflation, U.S. Economic Data\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gerald Jeffris  \par \uc2 378 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2013\par 20:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA--Brazil's real lost ground against the dollar to end weaker Thursday as tame local inflation and strong growth figures in the U.S. weighed against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The real exited regular trading at BRL2.3048 to the dollar after ending at BRL2.2811 to the dollar Wednesday, according to Tullett Prebon via Factset.\par \par \uc2 Traders noted the real began the session slipping against the dollar after Brazil's IBGE statistics institute reported October IPCA consumer price inflation of 0.57%, slightly lower than median market expectations.\par \par \uc2 The latest figure put Brazil's 12-month inflation at 5.84%, its lowest level since December, and reinforced expectations that the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would maintain its current path of tightening interest rates without having to resort to more aggressive rate increases.\par \par \uc2 The real, meanwhile got a jolt later in the session after the U.S. Commerce Department reported economic growth of 2.8% in the third quarter, a pace above market forecasts of around 2.0%,\par \par \uc2 The result was taken as a signal among many in the market that the U.S. Federal Reserve may have to act sooner rather than later in unwinding its long-running loose monetary policy and that dollar outflows will react accordingly.\par \par \uc2 "Capital outflows are already rising in anticipation of a Fed decision to begin reducing its monetary stimulus program," said Sidnei Nehme, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at the NGO brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 The fallout from the U.S. economic data, meanwhile, also overshadowed the latest signals from \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it may extend its current \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market through 2014 if necessary to provide dollar liquidity.\par \par \uc2 "We're keeping the possibility open," said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  economic policy director Carlos Hamilton on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The bank in August announced it would offer some $100 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and dollar credit lines to the market through the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintained its planned daily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with an auction of 10,000 swap contracts worth $496.6 million. \par \par \uc2 Write to Gerald Jeffris at gerald.jeffris@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 07, 2013 14:07 ET (19:07 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131107e9b7003yl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
