\par\par \b \uc2 Brazilian Real Falls on Deficit, U.S. Fed Worries\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paulo Winterstein  \par \uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2013\par 19:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--Brazil's real was one of the biggest losers against the dollar on Tuesday as global concerns that the U.S. Federal Reserve will start to withdraw its easy-money policies were compounded by concerns that the Latin American government isn't making serious efforts to curtail spending.\par \par \uc2 A number of emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have slipped against the dollar as investors are once again betting the Fed could start tapering its bond-buying program--which made borrowing in dollars less expensive and as a result has boosted the prices of overseas assets, including \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in emerging markets. Some investors expect the Fed to slow its bond-buying program as soon as next month, and consensus is growing that the Fed will start unwinding the program early next year.\par \par \uc2 The main losers so far have been \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the South African rand, the Turkish lira and the Hungarian forint, according to Dirk Willer, managing director for emerging-market strategy at Citibank. "But Brazil also is showing up," Mr. Willer said, in part because of a "further deterioration of the fiscal story."\par \par \uc2 Last week, the central government reported a surprisingly large deficit for September, at 10.47 billion Brazilian reais ($4.66 billion), nearly three times the figure economists had been expecting, which the government attributed to an unusual surge in social-security payments. The government is widely expected to miss its savings target for this year, while the amount of government debt as a ratio of gross domestic product may start to rise.\par \par \uc2 "Nobody in the government is talking seriously about more austere fiscal policy," said Paulo Nepomuceno, fixed-income strategist at Coinvalores brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 At 4:44 p.m. GMT, the real was trading at BRL2.2831, about 1.8% weaker than the close on Monday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has weakened nearly 6% since Oct. 17.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's Finance Minister Guido Mantega has in recent days addressed the issue, saying he wants to keep spending under control.\par \par \uc2 "The government is concerned about containing the rise in spending; that is a permanent preoccupation," Mr. Mantega told reporters on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Yet in a report in the Folha de S. Paulo newspaper on Tuesday, Gleisi Hoffman, President Dilma Rousseff's chief of staff, was quoted as saying she wants more flexibility on targets for government spending, which heightened the fears. Concern about public spending is especially crucial ahead of next year's election for president and state governors, when investors widely expect the government to loosen its purse strings even more.\par \par \uc2 The worries have already led one of the three major credit ratings firms to express a negative outlook for the Brazil's rating, raising the possibility of a downgrade. Earlier this year, \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  lowered its outlook on Brazil's triple-B investment grade debt to negative, citing slow economic growth and "continued expansionary fiscal policy." A lower credit rating makes it more expensive to borrow money.\par \par \uc2 Still, Mr. Nepomuceno said he doesn't expect a "meltdown" in the Brazilian real because \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets should be able to sustain the real at levels stronger than 2.35 reais per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already committed to daily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -futures market. That daily mechanism was introduced in August, when the real touched 2.45 per dollar amid a previous round of fears about the withdrawal of Fed policy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Willer said that some of the real's weakness is also due to the fact that investors were caught out by the suddenness of the real's move against the dollar, as they had been expecting the real to remain in a narrow range for some time, subdued by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Matthew Cowley contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Paulo Winterstein at paulo.winterstein@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 05, 2013 13:06 ET (18:06 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131105e9b500373\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
