\par\par \b \uc2 Strong Real May Lead  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  to Reduce  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,407 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2013\par 17:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:19 EDT - The Brazilian real has appreciated about 10% against the US dollar since the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began an effort on August 22 to support the real against the greenback through daily auctions of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and US dollar credit lines. But the real could already be too strong, reducing the competitiveness of Brazilian exports, say economists at private bank Itau. Consequently, the economists see a strong possibility the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will begin to quietly ease off on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not by suspending the program of auctions but, rather, by reducing the volume of swaps and credits it sells to the market. USD/BRL last off 0.2% to 2.179. (tom.murphy@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1504 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] ING Bank expects gross government bond issuance by euro zone sovereigns to fall in 2014 versus 2013, as a decline in funding needs due to shrinking budget deficits offsets a rise in redemptions. ING Bank forecasts gross bond issuance of EUR 864 billion, EUR 25 billion lower than in 2013, says senior rates strategist Alessandro Giansanti. Net issuance--gross issuance stripped of redemptions--will fall by a "notable" EUR 87 billion to EUR 218 billion in 2014 versus this year, Mr. Giansanti estimates. Broken down into individual countries, ING expects Germany to benefit from the biggest plunge in gross bond supply, to EUR 150 billion from EUR 183 billion, while Italy will face the largest spike in redemptions, to EUR 192 billion in 2014 from EUR 155 billion in 2013. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:49 EDT - BMO says Canada existing-home sales for September suggest worries about a hard landing in Canadian housing have become a "faint memory." Sales rose 0.8% last month, somewhat better than expected, though cooler than the 2.6%-per-month pace seen over the prior four months. The big 18.2% gain from Sept. 2012 reflects the sharp slump in sales last year after the government tightened mortgage rules, BMO says. "Underlying conditions are more balanced than the flashy headline results suggest," it adds. BMO has a track record of being pretty positive on the market in the last few months. Sept. data so far bear them out, but won't completely dispel fears of an eventual hard landing. (don.curren@wsj.com @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 9:58 EDT - There's worry among investors that Congress will test the market's resilience past Thursday, knowing that isn't the drop-dead date which would leave the US with nothing to pay its bills. Treasury has said it would still have about $30B to work with as of then, which alongside day-to-day revenue should last the US about 2 more weeks. But missed payments or not, the mainstream view is that Oct. 17 is the deadline, and market sentiment can quickly sour at the stroke of midnight without a deal. Now just 2 days away, the latest chatter is that the Senate's short-term funding plan faces tweaks from House Republicans. Treasurys are paring losses, with 10-year notes down just 2/32 to yield 2.698%. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 1358 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Corporate hybrid bonds issued more than a year ago may provide more upside than ones sold in recent months, say \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  credit analysts. The analysts prefer older, low-beta hybrid bonds as they offer a more attractive premium to senior bonds than new issues. "Many vintage hybrids will become callable in the next 2-3 years. We think issuers are likely to call them, to avoid higher rates and step-ups" the analysts say. RBS is long euro hybrids issued by RWE, EnBW, Suez, Vinci, OMV, \uc2 Dong Energy\uc2  (2015 call), \uc2 Veolia\uc2  and Linde. The bank is also long \uc2 Veolia\uc2 's sterling hybrid. (ben.edwards@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1354 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--The U.S. dollar remains burdened as long as uncertainty around a compromise in the country's budget and debt limit dispute continues, Raiffeisen Research says Tuesday. The bank adds the longer the wait for a solution, the more pressure there will be on the dollar. Should an agreement between the two political parties be reached, Raiffeisen says EUR/USD is likely to fall to 1.34. In the case that an agreement is not reached until Oct. 17, Raiffeisen forecasts prices between 1.36 and 1.37. If the Oct. 17 deadline is noticeably overshot, Raiffeisen expects temporary prices up to 1.40. (nicole.lundeen@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:48 EDT - Brazilian real's nearly 8% slide against the US dollar so far in 2013 isn't affecting retail sales, says IBGE researcher Aleciana Gusmao. Retail sales posted an unexpected 0.9% gain in August, second-consecutive monthly surprise. Gusmao points to, for example, computer prices rising at less than the inflation rate. Researcher also sees little upswing in segments vulnerable to exchange rates, such as sales of furniture, domestic appliances, office equipment and supermarkets. Higher wheat prices were offset by declines in other food categories, while tax breaks on furniture, domestic appliances also countered price gains in imported goods used in manufacturing, Gusmao adds. (jeff.fick@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1344 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--The year-end fair value of German two-year Treasury notes, also known as Schatz, is likely 0.30%, says \uc2 Barclays\uc2  strategist Huw Worthington. Schatz yields have sold off some 4 basis points since the end of last week, and sit around the mid-point of their 3-month trading range, he says. "This, however, is still some way below where we expect to see fair value for two-year Schatz yields by year-end, at 30 basis points currently," Mr. Worthington says. Germany will auction EUR5 billion of the 0.25% September 2015 Schatz on Wednesday. This Schatz is trading at a yield of 0.18%, according to Tradeweb, down from an average yield of 0.22% at its last auction on Sept. 18. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1309 GMT Providing some light relief to the usual drab reasons trotted out for consumer price trends, it appears that Poland's Sept CPI figure (1.0%, vs market expectation of 1.1%) has been tangibly affected by the givernment decision to slash fees for state-run kindergartens, starting in September, by some 17.7%. The cut is responsible for nearly 0.1% decline in CPI, Millennium says. The broker adds that September net inflation, due to be released tomorrow at 1200GMT, will also ease, to 1.3% from 1.4%. EUR/PLN trades at 4.1728. (patryk.wasilewski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1306 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Fund manager Franklin Templeton has reduced its holdings of French bonds in recent months, preferring Italian paper, its head of European fixed income, David Zahn, tells WSJ in an interview. "One market that is starting to concern us is France. Market participants seem to think it's very similar to Germany in being part of the euro-zone core. I'm not sure if that's the case," Mr. Zahn says. He adds that, in general, if Franklin Templeton doesn't like a particular country's debt, it doesn't own it, which leaves open the possibility of additional French debt sales. The 10-year French bond yield is 3bps higher at 2.40%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:010 EDT - Treasury bills due after October 17 have declined in yield from last week's lofty levels of a nearly a 40 basis point premium. As a result a more positive view on the dollar has come across markets which should continue unless the House rejects the plan and thrusts the Street back into a state of confusion. (vincent.cignarella@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 08:58 EDT - Yields on one-month Treasury bills fell in early trade as traders bet on Senate lawmakers reaching a deal to end the government shutdown and avoid a US debt default. Investors have been pulling back from bills maturing in October and November in recent days amid growing fears that the \uc2 US government\uc2  may run out of cash to repay some pieces of debt due in the coming weeks. Some of those trades are being unwounded this morning. One-month bills currently yield 0.185%, down from 0.271% earlier this the morning. (carolyn.cui@wsj.com) [ 15-10-13 1519GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020131015e9af000jf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
