\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Strong Real May Lead  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  to Reduce  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,484 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2013\par 17:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:19 EDT - The Brazilian real has appreciated about 10% against the US dollar since the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began an effort on August 22 to support the real against the greenback through daily auctions of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and US dollar credit lines. But the real could already be too strong, reducing the competitiveness of Brazilian exports, say economists at private bank Itau. Consequently, the economists see a strong possibility the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will begin to quietly ease off on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not by suspending the program of auctions but, rather, by reducing the volume of swaps and credits it sells to the market. USD/BRL last off 0.2% to 2.179. (tom.murphy@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:49 EDT - BMO says Canada existing-home sales for September suggest worries about a hard landing in Canadian housing have become a "faint memory." Sales rose 0.8% last month, somewhat better than expected, though cooler than the 2.6%-per-month pace seen over the prior four months. The big 18.2% gain from Sept. 2012 reflects the sharp slump in sales last year after the government tightened mortgage rules, BMO says. "Underlying conditions are more balanced than the flashy headline results suggest," it adds. BMO has a track record of being pretty positive on the market in the last few months. Sept. data so far bear them out, but won't completely dispel fears of an eventual hard landing. (don.curren@wsj.com @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 10:44 EDT - Citi's (C) 3Q mortgage banking revenue plunged 76% from the year-earlier quarter and 72% from the prior quarter to $167M. Meanwhile, mortgage originations at $14.5B were flat from the year ago, but down 16% from 2Q. CFO \uc2 John Gerspach\uc2  says the mortgage-revenue drops are mainly due to lower "saleable mortgage rate locks." Such rate-locks fall in value for banks as interest rates rise, adds Sandler O'Neill analyst Jeff Harte. C down 0.2% at $49.52. (saabira.chaudhuri@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:34 EDT - While "a more-defensive hardlines segment, well insulated from competitive threats," Wedbush has its concerns about auto-parts retailers as it starts coverage on the sector. "Despite favorable positioning, DIY auto stocks' glory days are no more" as "store traffic is an issue that is only going to get worse with shifting vehicle demographics" while earnings growth slows and return on capital stalls. But the investment bank does find reason to start O'Reilly (ORLY) at outperform as it "offers the most-attractive and visible growth profile, driven by new stores." \uc2 AutoZone\uc2  (AZO) and NAPA parent \uc2 Genuine Parts\uc2  (GPC) get neutrals and Advance Auto receives an underperform, with Wedbush calling it "a company in transition." While ORLY is only fractionally lower, the others drop 1%. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:26 EDT - Don't get skewed by Citi's (C) headline results falling short of consensus views, says SunTrust. The investment bank, which had below-Street estimates on the larger peer yet has been bullish on the stock, says C in 3Q showed "progress on 3 of the 4 initiatives that underpin" its investment stance. They are Citi Holdings' continuing to shrink as a percentage of C's balance sheet, capital ratios improving further--"positioning it for significant capital return beginning next year"--and logging core loan growth. "While operating leverage was negative...reflecting the weak revenue environment, it nonetheless remains positive" for the year, says SunTrust. C is now down just 0.3%, barely lagging the broader market. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:23 EDT - In SunTrust Robinson Humphrey's initial read on \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 's (C) 3Q, firm says revenue weakness masked strategic progress in three of four areas that support SunTrust's bullish view. Firm says C "continued to diminish the size of CitiHoldings as a percent of the overall balance sheet"; continued to improve capital ratios; and generated core loan growth. Negatives included weaker-than-expected revenue, "reflecting slightly more NIM compression than we had forecast, and lower investment banking income." Shares down a fraction at $49.44. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:19 EDT - FLIR is losing a fair amount of its big 2013 stock gains today after late Monday's warning, but Sterne Agee calls the delays behind it "a short-term overhang." The sensor-system maker is being hit by red tape at the federal government as well as the partial shutdown, impacting surveillance and related segments. But since orders haven't been cancelled, Sterne Agee says things will eventually catch up. The investment bank adds the restructuring concurrently announced by new CEO Andy Teich is him "putting his stamp on how FLIR will position itself to address the next leg" of growth on the commercial side. "We are aggressive buyers of FLIR on this weakness." It's down 9.2% to $30.12, cutting the year's gain to 35%. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:59 EDT - While still low versus peers, Jefferies notes 3Q purchases on Citi's (C) credit cards grew more versus a year ago than they did in the spring, improving for a second-straight quarter. The investment bank, which notes the results are a better read on \uc2 MasterCard\uc2  (MA) than Visa (V), are "an incremental positive read thru for networks/processors." Coming tomorrow will be \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  (BAC), \uc2 US Bancorp\uc2  (USB) and \uc2 American Express\uc2  (AXP), after which greater clarity on the payment processors and network operators should be clearer, says Jefferies. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:58 EDT - There's worry among investors that Congress will test the market's resilience past Thursday, knowing that isn't the drop-dead date which would leave the US with nothing to pay its bills. Treasury has said it would still have about $30B to work with as of then, which alongside day-to-day revenue should last the US about 2 more weeks. But missed payments or not, the mainstream view is that Oct. 17 is the deadline, and market sentiment can quickly sour at the stroke of midnight without a deal. Now just 2 days away, the latest chatter is that the Senate's short-term funding plan faces tweaks from House Republicans. Treasurys are paring losses, with 10-year notes down just 2/32 to yield 2.698%. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 09:48 EDT - Brazilian real's nearly 8% slide against the US dollar so far in 2013 isn't affecting retail sales, says IBGE researcher Aleciana Gusmao. Retail sales posted an unexpected 0.9% gain in August, second-consecutive monthly surprise. Gusmao points to, for example, computer prices rising at less than the inflation rate. Researcher also sees little upswing in segments vulnerable to exchange rates, such as sales of furniture, domestic appliances, office equipment and supermarkets. Higher wheat prices were offset by declines in other food categories, while tax breaks on furniture, domestic appliances also countered price gains in imported goods used in manufacturing, Gusmao adds. (jeff.fick@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:38 EDT - \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 's (C) $675M loan loss release comes entirely from its Citi Holdings division, with Citicorp building its release by $4M for the quarter. CFO \uc2 John Gerspach\uc2  says Citicorp has built loan-loss reserves in the international arms of its consumer business, which was somewhat driven by portfolio growth but also by a loan-loss pool that Citi has built in Latin America in 3Q for its exposure to home builders. Meanwhile, on the corporate side, Gerspach says Citi built some level of reserves due to increases in commitments and migration of certain credits to lower risk ratings during the quarter. Shares down 1.3% at $48.95. (saabira.chaudhuri@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:37 EDT - While Domino's (DPZ) posted its 2nd earnings miss in 2 years with its 3Q results thanks to soft margins, \uc2 Miller Tabak\uc2  is looking at the top line. DPZ's 5.5% increase in domestic same-store sales is much better than Pizza Hut's 1% drop, while DPZ's international locations are now 1 quarter shy of 20 straight years of SSS growth. But the investment bank also points out the surge in DPZ shares the past 16 months, and with sales comparisons getting harder starting this quarter amid the company's pan-pizza introduction, "we prefer to await a more-significant pullback." Shares are down 4.6% at $65.72. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 15, 2013 11:19 ET (15:19 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131015e9af0044d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
