\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Brazil's Mantega urges Fed to communicate tapering 'clearly'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Silvio Cascione and Patricia Duarte   \par \uc2 864 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2013\par 20:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments on exchange rate, interest rates)\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Oct 10 (Reuters) - The U.S. Federal Reserve should communicate clearly how it will phase out economic stimulus over the coming months to minimize market instability and prevent problems for emerging economies, Brazil's Finance Minister Guido Mantega said in an interview on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The stimulus reduction should also be carried out "very gradually," Mantega said, noting that the nomination of \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  to replace Fed Chairman \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2  appears to be a step in that direction.\par \par \uc2 Mantega, who skipped this week's \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  and G20 meetings in Washington to focus on domestic issues, said he believes that higher interest rates in Brazil will not be a drag on economic growth. With over $370 billion in international reserves, Brazil is also very well prepared to weather any market instability, Mantega said.\par \par \uc2 He added that leading emerging economies should have time to set up a joint reserve fund next year before the prospect of higher interest rates in the United States rekindles volatility.\par \par \uc2 "More clarity and more precision as the stimulus is reduced. This is what we've been recommending," Mantega said in the interview in his Sao Paulo office.\par \par \uc2 "If they announce: 'we are going to cut it by $10 billion a month; instead of $85 billion it will be $75 billion,' such a decision would clearly have no effect" on markets, he said.\par \par \uc2 Signs that the Fed could start tapering its $85-billion-a-month asset purchases in September sent shockwaves through world financial markets earlier this year, causing steep losses in emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Brazilian real .\par \par \uc2 Yellen, who next year will become the first woman ever to lead the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is a strong advocate of the Fed's aggressive actions to support economic growth through low interest rates and asset purchases.\par \par \uc2 She and fellow policymakers may need to keep the stimulus at full gear for an even longer period as the current political deadlock in Washington hurts economic growth, Mantega said.\par \par \uc2 The United States looks poised to avert a default, Mantega added, which would likely leave the next round of heightened market volatility for when a Yellen-led Fed starts raising interest rates in a few years.\par \par \uc2 "I believe \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  will continue \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2 's policies," Mantega said. "There is an excess of monetary stimulus and at some point this will need to be reduced. The right way to do it is in a very gradual way."\par \par \uc2 EXCHANGE RATE HELPING EXPORTERS\par \par \uc2 If Mantega's prediction pans out, leading emerging economies should have time to create a joint reserve fund to streamline \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by their central banks in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 The reserve fund of the BRICS group - including Brazil, Russia, India, China and South Africa - should be created by March with $100 billion in reserves, Mantega said.\par \par \uc2 He noted that the fund will need to be ratified by local parliaments then.\par \par \uc2 Mantega, Latin America's longest serving finance minister, has been a fierce critic of ultra-loose monetary policies in developed economies. He coined the term "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars" in 2010 to describe what he saw as an attempt by countries like the United States to devalue their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  through very low interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Brazil reacted with several capital controls, but began unwinding some of them this year as market concerns over the U.S. stimulus tapering sent the real to a near five-year low in August. Now at 2.2 per dollar and kept in a tight trading range by daily \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exchange rate is helping local industries, Mantega said.\par \par \uc2 He stressed, however, that the government does not seek to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any particular level.\par \par \uc2 "What I can say is that the exchange rate, at this level, is supporting exports, because I can see an improvement in exports of manufactured goods," he said.\par \par \uc2 HIGHER INTEREST RATES ARE NO TABOO\par \par \uc2 Mantega also said there is no "political taboo" preventing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from raising interest rates back to double-digit levels if needed to fight inflation.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave no sign it would stop raising its base policy rate in coming months after lifting it for a fifth straight time to 9.50 percent, the highest among the world's largest economies.\par \par \uc2 The increase should have little impact on economic growth as real interest rates remain historically low, Mantega said.\par \par \uc2 One measure of real interest rates is the base lending rate adjusted for inflation. In Brazil, it is currently around 3-4 percent.\par \par \uc2 "What is important is that we are not returning to what we had in the past. Brazil used to have nominal interest rates as high as 40 percent," Mantega said.\par \par \uc2 "It doesn't seem necessary for us to have 40-percent interest rates again, or 8-percent real interest rates." (Additional reporting by Cesar Bianconi; Editing by \uc2 Richard Chang\uc2  and Andrew Hay)\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL-ECONOMY/MANTEGA (INTERVIEW, UPDATE 2) | C | D | E | M | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | G | RBN | ABX | BNX | FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020131010e9aa0010i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
